{ id: "third-party-audit", section: "starters", name: "Annual independent compliance audit",
  practice: "Once a year, an outside auditor checks whether the lab actually did what its safety framework promised. A summary is made public.",
  why: "An audit looks back at the past year, so nothing waits on it. The effect is under a day.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Reps. Jay Obernolte and Lori Trahan", "what": "introduced the FRONTIER Act with independent audits, July 2026.", "url": "https://obernolte.house.gov/media/press-releases/obernolte-trahan-introduce-bipartisan-frontier-act-strengthen-oversight"}, {"who": "Foundation for American Innovation", "what": "called the FRONTIER Act Congress's best AI bill yet, 2026.", "url": "https://www.thefai.org/posts/the-frontier-act-is-congress-s-best-ai-bill-yet"}, {"who": "Asm. Rebecca Bauer-Kahan", "what": "wrote California's AI auditor registry law, saying industry can't grade its own homework, 2026.", "url": "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/09/governor-newsom-signs-first-in-the-nation-ai-safeguards-to-protect-californians-calls-on-the-federal-government-to-do-its-part/"}, {"who": "OpenAI", "what": "called California's audit laws a step in the right direction but prefers a federal rule, 2026.", "url": "https://www.ciodive.com/news/California-AI-auditing-legislation/830104/"}], "against": [{"who": "TechNet", "what": "opposed an earlier version of California's auditor registry as premature and deficient.", "url": "https://iapp.org/news/a/a-view-from-dc-what-will-all-these-ai-auditors-be-auditing"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/third-party-audit.html",
  short: "An outside auditor checks once a year that you did what your framework says.",
  trigger: "Covered frontier developers (SB 1047 would have started this in 2026).",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 6e-05, central: 0.00061, high: 0.001 }, d_access: { low: 0.00118, central: 0.00313, high: 0.00597 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "Whether audit findings ever require remediation before a launch.",
  requires: ["Retain an independent third-party auditor annually to assess compliance with the safety framework, identify gaps and recommend corrections (SB 1047 § 22603(e)).", "Publish a redacted audit report and send it to the Attorney General.", "California's 2026 laws SB 813 and AB 1405 set up a framework and registry for independent verification organizations."],
  baseline: "Labs already commission external security audits and, increasingly, external reviews of their safety frameworks. Anthropic commits to seeking public external review of its Risk Reports.",
  reasoning: `Annual audit findings do not delay every launch. The public-model stream uses a cycle-average approximation; dated checks in the supplied helper verify that an expired hold does not keep delaying later releases.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `An auditor who has to sign off on a framework may demand conservative interpretations of thresholds, which ratchets a lab's own gates tighter. Over several generations that ratchet could add up. It is a second-order effect and we have no data on it.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "SB 1047 enrolled text, § 22603(e): third-party audits", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB1047" },
    { title: "Governor Newsom signs SB 813 and AB 1405 (Sept 9, 2026)", url: "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/09/governor-newsom-signs-first-in-the-nation-ai-safeguards-to-protect-californians-calls-on-the-federal-government-to-do-its-part/" }
  ] },

{ id: "internal-evals", section: "mains", name: "Mandatory internal capability evaluations",
  practice: "Before release, the lab must test every frontier model for dangerous abilities, such as helping build a bioweapon or break into computer systems, and record the results.",
  why: "Labs already run these tests, mostly on earlier versions of the model. Making them mandatory adds almost nothing to the lab's own progress; the public launch waits a few days.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Shevlane et al.", "what": "researchers argued dangerous-capability evaluations should drive training and release decisions, 2023.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15324"}, {"who": "EU Code of Practice", "what": "requires state-of-the-art evaluations before a model is placed on the market, 2025.", "url": "https://code-of-practice.ai/?section=safety-security"}, {"who": "California SB 1047", "what": "would have required a critical-harm assessment before release, 2024.", "url": "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB1047"}], "against": [{"who": "Meta", "what": "refused to sign the EU code, calling it overreach, 2025.", "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/18/meta-refuses-to-sign-eus-ai-code-of-practice"}, {"who": "Dean Ball, Foundation for American Innovation", "what": "called a federal pre-release evaluation mandate a federally mandated veto point, 2025.", "url": "https://reason.com/2025/10/02/josh-hawley-and-democrat-allies-target-ai-with-new-legal-and-regulatory-regime/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/internal-evals.html",
  short: "Run dangerous-capability evaluations on every frontier model before you deploy it.",
  trigger: "Frontier models before external deployment.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00093, high: 0.00153 }, d_access: { low: 0.00275, central: 0.00789, high: 0.01847 },
  confidence: "Medium-high", evidence: "high",
  uncertainty: "How much of the evaluation must wait for the final checkpoint rather than a proxy.",
  requires: ["Before deployment, run a defined suite of evaluations for chemical, biological, cyber and autonomy capabilities and document the results.", "Compare results against published thresholds and record the deployment decision."],
  baseline: "OpenAI's Preparedness Framework says every covered model undergoes its suite of scalable evaluations prior to deployment. Anthropic runs capability assessments under its RSP. GPT-5.5 went through targeted red-teaming for cyber and biology before release. The requirement is already the norm.",
  reasoning: `Published testing durations anchor possible workloads; only mandatory work beyond the voluntary suite is incremental. Most testing overlaps training, launch preparation and other safety work. Findings requiring new remediation belong to the separate triggered-remediation mechanism.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Evaluation suites grow. A mandate with a fixed list could add human uplift studies or agentic evaluations that take weeks and cannot run on proxies. And a legal duty to evaluate is a legal duty to act on the result, which turns a measurement into a gate. That gate is priced separately, but a critic would say the two cannot be separated in practice.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "AISI, Early lessons from evaluating frontier AI systems", url: "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/early-lessons-from-evaluating-frontier-ai-systems", note: "light tier: few days, 1–2 weeks end to end; standard: 1–2 weeks; elevated: several weeks" },
    { title: "OpenAI Preparedness Framework v2", url: "https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/18a02b5d-6b67-4cec-ab64-68cdfbddebcd/preparedness-framework-v2.pdf", note: "every covered model undergoes scalable evaluations prior to deployment" },
    { title: "GPT-5.5 System Card", url: "https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-5-system-card/", note: "full pre-deployment suite plus targeted red-teaming" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Labs already run these, mostly on earlier versions of the model. Only the public launch waits." },

{ id: "third-party-eval", section: "mains", name: "Independent pre-deployment evaluation",
  practice: "An approved outside group, such as a government AI safety institute, tests the model before the public can use it and reports what it found.",
  why: "The lab keeps using and improving the model while outsiders test it, so the U.S. frontier barely moves. The public launch waits, which slows China only if it depends on U.S. models.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Sens. Hawley and Blumenthal", "what": "would bar release until a Department of Energy evaluation is done, 2025.", "url": "https://www.hawley.senate.gov/hawley-blumenthal-introduce-bipartisan-ai-evaluation-legislation-to-put-americans-first"}, {"who": "U.S. AI Safety Institute, now CAISI", "what": "signed agreements for access to Anthropic and OpenAI models before release, 2024.", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2024/08/us-ai-safety-institute-signs-agreements-regarding-ai-safety-research"}, {"who": "UK and U.S. AI Safety Institutes", "what": "jointly tested Claude 3.5 Sonnet before its launch, 2024.", "url": "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/work/pre-deployment-evaluation-of-anthropics-upgraded-claude-3-5-sonnet"}, {"who": "Americans for Responsible Innovation", "what": "backed the Hawley–Blumenthal testing bill, 2025.", "url": "https://ari.us/sen-hawley-introduces-ai-oversight-legislation/"}], "against": [{"who": "Dean Ball, Foundation for American Innovation", "what": "called mandatory federal testing a veto point on the future of AI, 2025.", "url": "https://reason.com/2025/10/02/josh-hawley-and-democrat-allies-target-ai-with-new-legal-and-regulatory-regime/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/third-party-eval.html",
  short: "An approved outside evaluator tests the model and reports before public release.",
  trigger: "Frontier models before external deployment.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00078, high: 0.00123 }, d_access: { low: 0.00357, central: 0.00847, high: 0.01874 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "high",
  uncertainty: "Whether labs would share checkpoints earlier or simply wait longer at the end.",
  requires: ["Give an independent evaluator (a government institute or an approved organization) access to a representative checkpoint before release.", "Receive the evaluator's report before public deployment; the report can be redacted for publication.", "No fixed access period is set; the evaluator gets what it gets."],
  baseline: "Every major U.S. release now goes through some external testing. UK AISI and U.S. CAISI tested GPT-5.5. METR had ten business days on Claude Opus 5.5 and three weeks on o3 and GPT-5. But access is uneven: Apollo had three days on GPT-6 Astra and SecureBio six calendar days. The practice exists; the mandate makes it universal and makes the report a precondition.",
  reasoning: `Qualifying evaluations already conducted voluntarily are reused. External evaluator service, queueing, existing access runway and final-report tails are distinct. This is not a claim that every observed evaluation caused its entire elapsed duration as delay.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Evaluators are capacity-constrained. If every frontier release from four labs must queue for the same institute, the queue is the delay, and it lands in the lab's release schedule whether or not the model is done. METR itself says deployment-tied evaluations often leave little time for thorough analysis. A mandate that fixes that problem by adding time is, by construction, adding time. And the labs' own behavior suggests they treat evaluator windows as release-blocking rather than parallel.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "GPT-5.5 System Card: external evaluations", url: "https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-5-5", note: "SecureBio April 2–9; UK AISI and CAISI pre-deployment testing" },
    { title: "GPT-6 Astra deployment safety page", url: "https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-astra", note: "Apollo: three days" },
    { title: "METR evaluation of Claude Opus 5.5", url: "https://metr.org/blog/2026-09-22-claude-opus-5-5/", note: "10 business days of API access" },
    { title: "METR evaluation of o3 and o4-mini", url: "https://metr.org/evaluations/openai-o3-report/", note: "three weeks prior to release" },
    { title: "METR Frontier Risk Report, Feb–Mar 2026", url: "https://metr.org/blog/2026-05-19-frontier-risk-report/", note: "pre-deployment evaluations often leave little time" },
    { title: "AISI, Early lessons from evaluating frontier AI systems", url: "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/early-lessons-from-evaluating-frontier-ai-systems" },
    { title: "Expanding External Access to Frontier AI Models (arXiv 2601.11916)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11916", note: "Apollo and UK AISI had less than a week on Claude Sonnet 4.5" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "The model already exists when the evaluator gets it. Only the release clock moves." },

{ id: "evaluator-window", section: "mains", name: "Twenty business days of evaluator access",
  practice: "Outside testers get at least four weeks with a new model before the public can use it. Today they often get between three days and three weeks.",
  why: "Only the public launch waits. The lab keeps building on the model internally, so the lead barely moves. If China depends on U.S. models, the delay slows China slightly more than America.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "EU Code of Practice", "what": "says at least 20 business days is appropriate for most evaluations, 2025.", "url": "https://code-of-practice.ai/?section=safety-security"}, {"who": "METR", "what": "said its o3 evaluation was done in a relatively short time, over only three weeks, 2025.", "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/16/openai-partner-says-it-had-relatively-little-time-to-test-the-companys-new-ai-models/"}, {"who": "Charnock, Casper and colleagues", "what": "noted outside testers got about one week with Claude Sonnet 4 and 4.5 and argued for more, 2026.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2601.11916"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/evaluator-window.html",
  short: "Outside evaluators get at least four weeks with the model before it ships.",
  trigger: "Frontier models before external deployment (the EU Code of Practice's suggested period).",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00172, high: 0.00285 }, d_access: { low: 0.01068, central: 0.03139, high: 0.09845 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "high",
  uncertainty: "Whether labs respond by sharing checkpoints four weeks earlier or by shipping four weeks later.",
  requires: ["Provide independent external evaluators with at least 20 business days of access to the model before public release.", "The EU Code of Practice describes this as appropriate for most systemic risks and evaluation methods; METR's guidance for lab staff repeats it."],
  baseline: "Actual windows in 2025 and 2026 ran from three days (GPT-6 Astra, Apollo) to three weeks (o3, GPT-5). Ten business days on Claude Opus 5.5 is the recent norm at the generous end. A 20-business-day floor is two to three weeks more than typical practice.",
  reasoning: `Twenty business days is modeled as a 28-calendar-day convention. Existing access can satisfy much of that requirement. Earlier sharing is allowed; a material checkpoint reset can create an additional residual tail. Holidays and particular legal counting conventions are not separately forecast.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Four weeks is a real number in a field where METR measures the frontier doubling every 105 days. If commercial availability drives revenue, and revenue drives compute purchases, a systematic four-week delay on every release compounds into a slower frontier. And the internal-versus-public distinction may be thinner than it looks: OpenAI's internal-to-external gap is only a few weeks, so a four-week floor could exceed the lab's own cadence and force it to hold the internal frontier too.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "EU GPAI Code of Practice, Safety and Security chapter, Appendix 3.4", url: "https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/dae/redirection/document/118119", note: "at least 20 business days is appropriate for most systemic risks" },
    { title: "METR, Frontier AI safety regulations: a reference for lab staff", url: "https://metr.org/notes/2026-01-29-frontier-ai-safety-regulations/" },
    { title: "METR evaluation of Claude Opus 5.5", url: "https://metr.org/blog/2026-09-22-claude-opus-5-5/", note: "10 business days" },
    { title: "GPT-6 Astra deployment safety page", url: "https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-astra", note: "3 days for Apollo" },
    { title: "METR Frontier Risk Report PDF", url: "https://metr.org/risk-report-feb-mar-2026.pdf", note: "OpenAI's internal-to-external gap was a few weeks" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "A release-only gate. If China depends on U.S. models, it can even widen the lead." },

{ id: "triggered-redteam", section: "mains", name: "Triggered red-team and remediation",
  practice: "If testing shows a model crossing a danger line, such as giving real help with a bioweapon, the lab must have experts attack it hard and fix what they find before anyone uses it.",
  why: "It only applies when a model crosses a danger line, and most fixes are prepared in advance. Averaged over a year, the effect is under a day.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Anthropic", "what": "its Responsible Scaling Policy ties stronger safeguards to capability thresholds.", "url": "https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy"}, {"who": "OpenAI", "what": "its Preparedness Framework requires safeguards when a model reaches High capability.", "url": "https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/18a02b5d-6b67-4cec-ab64-68cdfbddebcd/preparedness-framework-v2.pdf"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/triggered-redteam.html",
  short: "If an evaluation crosses a danger threshold, do elevated testing and fix it before anyone can use it.",
  trigger: "Any frontier model whose evaluations cross a defined capability threshold.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "cond", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00174, central: 0.00483, high: 0.00808 }, d_access: { low: 0.00542, central: 0.01651, high: 0.02707 },
  confidence: "Low-medium", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How often thresholds get crossed and whether safeguards must be in place for internal use as well as release.",
  requires: ["When a pre-deployment evaluation crosses a capability threshold, conduct elevated testing (expert red-teaming, possibly uplift studies).", "Implement and verify safeguards before deployment; if the threshold governs internal use, before internal deployment too."],
  baseline: "Both major frameworks already have this shape. OpenAI's Preparedness Framework requires safeguards at High capability; its GPT-6 Astra card says safeguards were required even for internal deployment because of cyber capability. Anthropic's RSP has ASL-3 and ASL-4 safeguards tied to thresholds. Thresholds are now being crossed routinely rather than hypothetically.",
  reasoning: `The relevant probability is an incremental binding intervention, not merely a model crossing a risk threshold. Prepared mitigations reduce remaining work. Joint incidence and remedy difficulty could be more correlated than the base independent priors.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Thresholds only get crossed more often from here. If every frontier model now triggers elevated testing, the conditional becomes unconditional and this item becomes a standing four-to-six-week gate. Worse, if regulators rather than labs define the threshold and the remediation standard, the lab loses the ability to prepare safeguards in advance for a threshold it can anticipate. That would move the central price toward a month and push more of it onto the internal clock.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "AISI, Early lessons from evaluating frontier AI systems", url: "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/early-lessons-from-evaluating-frontier-ai-systems", note: "elevated tier: several weeks" },
    { title: "GPT-6 Astra deployment safety page", url: "https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-astra", note: "safeguards required even for internal deployment" },
    { title: "OpenAI Preparedness Framework v2", url: "https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/18a02b5d-6b67-4cec-ab64-68cdfbddebcd/preparedness-framework-v2.pdf" },
    { title: "Anthropic Responsible Scaling Policy", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "It only applies when a model crosses a danger line, and most fixes are prepared in advance." },

{ id: "safety-case-deploy", section: "mains", name: "Safety case before deployment",
  practice: "Before release, the lab writes a structured argument, backed by evidence, that the model is safe enough to launch. A reviewer has to approve it.",
  why: "Writing and reviewing the case takes senior time and holds up the public launch. The lab's own progress loses under a day; if China depends on U.S. models, the delayed launch slows China more.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Clymer, Krueger and colleagues", "what": "proposed safety cases to guide AI deployment decisions, 2024.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.10462"}, {"who": "UK AI Security Institute", "what": "builds safety case templates, 2024.", "url": "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/safety-cases-at-aisi"}, {"who": "GovAI", "what": "argued safety cases can support both self-regulation and government oversight, 2024.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21572"}, {"who": "Anthropic", "what": "said some of its commitments take the form of affirmative safety cases, 2024.", "url": "https://alignment.anthropic.com/2024/safety-cases/"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses about 2 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/safety-case-deploy.html",
  short: "Write a structured argument that the model is safe to release, and have it reviewed, before you release it.",
  trigger: "Frontier models before external deployment.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00073, central: 0.0183, high: 0.02915 }, d_access: { low: 0.09772, central: 0.24021, high: 0.47089 },
  confidence: "Medium-low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "Nobody has published how long a frontier safety case takes to write or review.",
  requires: ["Before external deployment, produce a safety case: a structured argument, with evidence, that the model's risks are below defined levels given its safeguards.", "Submit it to a regulator or independent reviewer and receive sign-off before release."],
  baseline: "Anthropic's Risk Reports are the closest published artifact. Under RSP v3.4 they go to external reviewers within a week of the Board, and reviewers are asked to respond within 30 days. OpenAI's Capabilities and Safeguards Reports serve a similar internal role. No lab currently waits for an outside sign-off before launch.",
  reasoning: `The long-tail approval model can materially defer external availability while having a much smaller effect on technical progress. Internal-use restrictions enter research productivity separately. On a publicly deployable-model metric, this policy would have a substantially larger positive delay.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is about 2 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 1 day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `We have no data on how long these take. AISI has written about safety cases as a method; no lab has published one for a frontier launch with a timeline. A regulator's review could take far longer than 30 days the first few times, and a lab that fails review restarts the clock. If the practical review tail is six weeks rather than two, this item costs a month and a half of release delay per generation, and if the lab holds internal rollout for the sign-off, it costs the same on the U.S. clock.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Anthropic RSP v3.4: external review of Risk Reports", url: "https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/files/4zrzovbb/website/0bacdc8440ea96e62a8766d99ebe1d4eea6d5f3a.pdf", note: "shared within one week; comments within 30 days" },
    { title: "AISI, How can safety cases be used to help with frontier AI safety?", url: "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/how-can-safety-cases-be-used-to-help-with-frontier-ai-safety" },
    { title: "OpenAI Preparedness Framework v2", url: "https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/18a02b5d-6b67-4cec-ab64-68cdfbddebcd/preparedness-framework-v2.pdf", note: "Capabilities and Safeguards Reports" }
  ] },

{ id: "safety-case-train", section: "specials", name: "Authorization before frontier training",
  practice: "Before starting a very large training run, the lab submits a safety plan to a regulator and waits for approval. The regulator can say no.",
  why: "This comes before the model exists, so waiting for approval delays the next training run. The model estimates about four weeks of lost U.S. progress a year after the rule takes effect.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Sens. Blumenthal and Hawley", "what": "proposed a licensing regime run by an independent oversight body, 2023.", "url": "https://www.blumenthal.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09072023bipartisanaiframework.pdf"}, {"who": "Sam Altman, OpenAI", "what": "told the Senate the U.S. should consider licensing for models above a capability threshold, 2023.", "url": "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2023-05-16%20-%20Bio%20&%20Testimony%20-%20Altman.pdf"}, {"who": "Brad Smith, Microsoft", "what": "called for licensing so an agency is informed of testing before deployment, 2023.", "url": "https://www.geekwire.com/2023/microsoft-calls-for-safety-brakes-licensing-and-a-new-federal-agency-to-avoid-ai-pitfalls/"}], "against": [{"who": "Andrew Ng", "what": "called AI licensing a massively, colossally dumb idea that would crush innovation, 2023.", "url": "https://siliconangle.com/2023/10/31/google-brain-founder-andrew-ng-says-threat-ai-causing-human-extinction-overblown/"}, {"who": "Yann LeCun, Meta", "what": "accused leading lab CEOs of attempting regulatory capture, 2023.", "url": "https://futurism.com/the-byte/godfather-of-ai-warns-control"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 4 weeks behind where it would be; China loses about 2 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/safety-case-train.html",
  short: "Get a regulator's approval before you start a training run above the threshold.",
  trigger: "Training runs above a compute threshold (10^26 FLOP or a successor).",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "yes", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 0.24901, central: 0.87656, high: 1.91492 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.20683, high: 0.35491 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether the regulator can say no, and how long it takes to say yes.",
  requires: ["Before beginning a training run above the threshold, submit a safety and security plan for the run and receive authorization.", "The regulator may impose conditions or withhold approval.", "Distinct from SB 1047, which required a self-certified protocol before training but no approval."],
  baseline: "No U.S. law does this. SB 1047 required a written protocol and cybersecurity before training, self-certified. The EU AI Act requires notification, not approval. A true licensing gate on training has been proposed by think tanks and in early drafts of federal bills but not enacted.",
  reasoning: `The model distinguishes raw authorization waiting from research preserved during waiting. Only serial stages count toward frontier delay; parallel training runs increase workload. The first affected checkpoint arrives after a pipeline delay, preserving existing models and ongoing runs.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 4 weeks behind its baseline path and China's is about 2 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 3.5 weeks, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Licensing regimes in other industries take far longer than 30 days once they mature, and a regulator that can say no will eventually say no. The first refusal costs a generation, not a month. A lab also cannot plan a run it does not know will be approved, so the parallel-filing assumption breaks down as the regime tightens. Three months could be the floor rather than the ceiling.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "SB 1047 enrolled text, § 22603(a): pre-training duties (self-certified)", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB1047" },
    { title: "EU AI Act, Article 52: notification obligations for GPAI with systemic risk", url: "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/52/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Upstream of the model. Delays every clock by the same amount." },

{ id: "training-notice-30", section: "specials", name: "Thirty-day pre-training notice",
  practice: "The lab tells the government 30 days before starting a very large training run. No approval is needed; the run can start when the 30 days are up.",
  why: "Most runs are planned well ahead, but plans change and a fresh 30-day clock sometimes delays a run. That costs about 3 days over a year.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "President Biden", "what": "required reports on planned training of the largest models, 2023.", "url": "https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/10/30/executive-order-on-the-safe-secure-and-trustworthy-development-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence/"}, {"who": "Commerce Department", "what": "proposed quarterly reports on training runs above 10^26 operations, 2024.", "url": "https://www.mayerbrown.com/en/insights/publications/2024/09/us-department-of-commerce-issues-proposal-to-require-reporting-development-of-advanced-ai-models-and-computer-clusters"}, {"who": "Sens. Romney, Reed, Moran and King", "what": "proposed notice before frontier training runs begin, 2024.", "url": "https://www.king.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/bipartisan_ai_framework_letter.pdf"}], "against": [{"who": "President Trump", "what": "revoked Biden's AI executive order on his first day, January 2025.", "url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/initial-rescissions-of-harmful-executive-orders-and-actions/"}, {"who": "Ben Horowitz, a16z", "what": "criticized regulating math, FLOPs and R&D, 2024.", "url": "https://time.com/6996927/republicans-repeal-biden-ai-executive-order/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 3 days behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/training-notice-30.html",
  short: "File a plan 30 days before a frontier run. No approval needed, but the clock runs.",
  trigger: "Training runs above a compute threshold.",
  clocks: { training: "cond", internal: "cond", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.02859, central: 0.09424, high: 0.20259 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.02219, high: 0.03764 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether labs actually know 30 days out that a run will happen.",
  requires: ["Notify the regulator at least 30 days before starting a training run above the threshold, with a description of the run and its safety plan.", "No approval is required; the run may proceed when the period ends."],
  baseline: "The 2023 U.S. executive order on AI required reporting on large training runs but was rescinded in January 2025. The EU AI Act requires notification within two weeks of a model meeting the systemic-risk threshold, which is after the fact. No current U.S. rule imposes a pre-run waiting period.",
  reasoning: `The statutory notice duration is not treated as 30 lost days for every training run. Planning lead time, material changes and work conducted in parallel determine the residual. Additional qualifying runs do not automatically become serial frontier delays.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 3 days behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 3 days, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `The frontier is increasingly made by many mid-sized runs, not one giant run per year. If the threshold catches routine runs, a 30-day notice on each is a standing tax on iteration speed. Labs would either batch experiments, which slows learning, or design around the threshold, which slows scale.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Executive Order 14110 (Oct 2023), § 4.2: reporting on dual-use foundation model training", url: "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2023/11/01/2023-24283/safe-secure-and-trustworthy-development-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence", note: "rescinded January 2025" },
    { title: "EU AI Act, Article 52: notification within two weeks", url: "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/52/" }
  ] },

{ id: "pause-6", section: "specials", name: "Six-month training pause",
  practice: "No one may train an AI system more powerful than today's best for at least six months. This is what the 2023 open letter asked for.",
  why: "Six months without large training runs. Research continues and labs partly catch up after the pause, so a year later America is about a month and a half behind where it would have been. At the pause's end the gap is larger.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Future of Life Institute", "what": "wrote the 2023 open letter calling for a pause of at least six months, now with more than 31,000 signatures.", "url": "https://futureoflife.org/open-letter/pause-giant-ai-experiments/"}, {"who": "Elon Musk, Steve Wozniak, Yoshua Bengio, Stuart Russell", "what": "were among the letter's best-known signers, 2023.", "url": "https://techxplore.com/news/2023-03-musk-scientists-halt-ai-chatgpt.html"}, {"who": "PauseAI", "what": "campaigns to stop development of the most powerful general AI systems until they can be made safe.", "url": "https://pauseai.info/"}, {"who": "Sen. Bernie Sanders and Rep. Greg Casar", "what": "introduced a bill to pause advanced AI development until a new federal regulator is running, September 2026.", "url": "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-casar-introduce-legislation-to-create-new-federal-agency-to-ban-artificial-superintelligence-pause-advanced-ai-development/"}], "against": [{"who": "Bill Gates", "what": "said asking one group to pause doesn't solve the challenges, 2023.", "url": "https://wsau.com/2023/04/04/bill-gates-says-calls-to-pause-ai-wont-solve-challenges/"}, {"who": "Yann LeCun and Andrew Ng", "what": "held a public talk titled Why the 6-month AI Pause is a Bad Idea, 2023.", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BY9KV8uCtj4"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 1.5 months behind where it would be; China loses about 7 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/pause-6.html",
  short: "The 2023 letter. No training of systems more powerful than the frontier for six months.",
  trigger: "Training of systems more powerful than the current frontier.",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "yes", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 0.98718, central: 1.67319, high: 2.5415 }, d_access: { low: 0.43349, central: 0.90113, high: 1.27059 },
  confidence: "High", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "Whether it ends when it says it ends.",
  requires: ["All AI labs immediately pause for at least six months the training of AI systems more powerful than GPT-4 (the frontier at the time).", "The pause should be public and verifiable and include all key actors.", "If it cannot be enacted quickly, governments should step in and institute a moratorium."],
  baseline: "Proposed on March 22, 2023 by the Future of Life Institute letter, which now shows more than 31,000 signatures. Not enacted anywhere. Training did not pause.",
  reasoning: `This is a six-month U.S.-only pause, with China continuing, as in the menu’s original pricing convention. Below-threshold research and permitted methods continue. Restarting partially recovers the scaling gap before month 12. The maximum loss during the pause differs from the endpoint loss.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 1.5 months behind its baseline path and China's is about 7 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 1.5 months, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `The letter asked for a pause by every key actor, including China. If that happened, the lead cost is zero by definition. It did not happen and would not. As a unilateral policy, this is the most expensive item on the menu and the steelman for a higher price is that "at least six months" has no upper bound.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Pause Giant AI Experiments: An Open Letter (March 2023)", url: "https://futureoflife.org/open-letter/pause-giant-ai-experiments/" }
  ] },

{ id: "compute-cap", section: "specials", name: "Hard cap on training compute",
  practice: "No training run may use more computing power than today's largest runs, and the limit never rises.",
  why: "A cap freezes the size of training runs while better algorithms keep coming. A year in, the U.S. frontier is about four months behind where it would have been.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "ControlAI, A Narrow Path", "what": "proposes compute limits and a ban on superintelligence for 20 years, 2024.", "url": "https://www.narrowpath.co/phase-0"}, {"who": "MIRI Technical Governance Team", "what": "drafted an international agreement banning training runs above 10^24 FLOP, 2025.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10783"}, {"who": "Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares", "what": "called for treaty monitoring of any cluster larger than eight top chips, 2025.", "url": "https://www.transformernews.ai/p/review-if-anyone-builds-it-everyone-dies-yudkowsky-soares"}, {"who": "Statement on Superintelligence", "what": "Hinton, Bengio, Wozniak, Bannon, Susan Rice and others called for a ban on superintelligence, 2025.", "url": "https://cyberscoop.com/ai-superintelligence-ban-open-letter-future-of-life-harry-meghan-tech-leaders/"}], "against": [{"who": "Daniel Castro, ITIF", "what": "called banning superintelligence a historic mistake, 2025.", "url": "https://itif.org/publications/2025/12/04/banning-ai-superintelligence-would-be-a-historic-mistake/"}, {"who": "Neil Chilson, Abundance Institute", "what": "called the treaty and GPU proposals astoundingly casual authoritarianism, 2026.", "url": "https://reason.com/2026/02/01/superintelligent-ai-is-not-coming-to-kill-you/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 4 months behind where it would be; China loses about 8 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/compute-cap.html",
  short: "No training run above a fixed ceiling set at about today's largest runs, and the ceiling never rises.",
  trigger: "All training runs above the cap (say 10^27 FLOP).",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "yes", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 2.69539, central: 4.19094, high: 5.86066 }, d_access: { low: 0.66357, central: 1.10303, high: 1.49665 },
  recurrence: "Per year the cap stays in place",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much frontier progress comes from compute scaling versus algorithms and post-training.",
  requires: ["Prohibit training runs above a fixed compute threshold.", "The threshold does not rise with time."],
  baseline: "No jurisdiction has a hard cap. Thresholds in SB 53 and the EU AI Act trigger obligations, not prohibitions.",
  reasoning: `A permanent ceiling progressively binds as baseline training compute grows. Reallocation to other experiments, algorithmic work and permitted inference/post-training recover part of the lost scale. The result is an endpoint shortfall, not a fixed annual bill to add forever.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 4 months behind its baseline path and China's is about 8 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 4 months, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Compute is the input the U.S. leads on. Epoch and RAND put the U.S. hardware lead at about four years and its compute capacity at roughly ten times China's. A cap surrenders the one advantage that export controls protect. The lead cost could be the entire lead within two years.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Epoch AI, U.S. export controls and China's AI progress", url: "https://epoch.ai/gradient-updates/us-export-controls-china-ai", note: "hardware lead of about four years" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Algorithmic progress in language models", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/algorithmic-progress-in-language-models", note: "60–95% of gains from compute and data" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Training compute of frontier AI models grows by 4-5x per year", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/training-compute-of-frontier-ai-models-grows-by-4-5x-per-year" },
    { title: "RAND (Heim), China's AI models are closing the gap", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/commentary/2025/05/chinas-ai-models-are-closing-the-gap-but-americas-real.html", note: "roughly ten times the compute capacity" }
  ] },

{ id: "internal-deploy", section: "mains", name: "Safety checks before internal use",
  practice: "Before a lab's own staff can use a new frontier model, for example to write code and run experiments, the lab must finish its dangerous-capability tests and put safeguards in place, the same way it would before a public launch.",
  why: "Researchers keep using the previous model while the new one is tested, so the loss is only the extra help the newest model would have given. About a day over a year.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Apollo Research", "what": "recommended extending frontier safety policies to internal deployment, 2025.", "url": "https://www.apolloresearch.ai/governance/ai-behind-closed-doors-a-primer-on-the-governance-of-internal-deployment"}, {"who": "California SB 53", "what": "requires frameworks to manage risk from internal use, 2025.", "url": "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53"}, {"who": "Joe Kwon and Stephen Casper", "what": "argued U.S. and EU rules leave internal deployment under-supervised, 2026.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08005"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 1 day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/internal-deploy.html",
  short: "Test and safeguard a model before the lab's own researchers use it, not just before the public does.",
  trigger: "Frontier models, at the point of internal deployment.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "yes", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.01143, central: 0.03986, high: 0.09369 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.01282, high: 0.02197 },
  recurrence: "Per model generation",
  confidence: "Medium-low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How long labs would hold a model back from their own staff while tests finish.",
  requires: ["Complete dangerous-capability evaluations before a frontier model is deployed inside the lab.", "Put required safeguards in place before internal use, not only before public release.", "California SB 53 already requires large developers to assess catastrophic risk from internal use and send the state summaries every three months."],
  baseline: "Labs already test models before release, and some safeguards now apply internally. OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra system card says its Preparedness Framework required safeguards even for internal deployment because of the model's cyber capabilities. California's SB 53 requires a framework for managing risk from internal use. What labs do not do is hold every new model back from their own staff until testing is done.",
  reasoning: `A newer model improves some research tasks over the predecessor already available. The model charges that incremental research productivity during the gate, using exposed task share and model cadence. It does not charge the same capability both as a calendar delay and as lost research output.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 1 day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 1 day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `As labs automate more of their own research, the internal window becomes the whole race. If a model has to wait for full testing before it can help build its successor, and testing keeps getting longer, the cost could reach a month or more per generation and compound.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Stix et al., AI Behind Closed Doors: a Primer on the Governance of Internal Deployment (Apollo Research, April 2025)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12170", note: "governance of internal deployment of highly advanced systems appears absent" },
    { title: "California SB 53 bill text", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53", note: "internal-use risk in the frontier AI framework; quarterly summaries" },
    { title: "OpenAI GPT-6 Astra system card: safeguards", url: "https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-astra/safeguards", note: "safeguards required even for internal deployment" },
    { title: "METR Frontier Risk Report, Feb–Mar 2026", url: "https://metr.org/blog/2026-05-19-frontier-risk-report/", note: "internal frontier about 66 days ahead of the public" },
    { title: "Anthropic, When AI builds itself", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/institute/recursive-self-improvement", note: "more than 80% of merged code authored by Claude as of May 2026" },
    { title: "UK AISI, Early lessons from evaluating frontier AI systems", url: "https://www.aisi.gov.uk/blog/early-lessons-from-evaluating-frontier-ai-systems" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Researchers keep using the previous model, so only the newest model's extra help is lost." },

{ id: "ai-rnd-limits", section: "specials", name: "Human sign-off on automated AI research",
  practice: "Labs may not let AI systems run AI research on their own from start to finish. A human researcher has to direct and approve the experiments AI agents run, and labs must report how much of their research AI is doing.",
  why: "Human sign-off caps how much AI can speed up research. AI's research boost is still modest, so the loss over a year is about 4 days.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "IDAIS-Beijing scientists", "what": "including Bengio said no AI should improve itself without explicit human approval, 2024.", "url": "https://idais.ai/dialogue/idais-beijing/"}, {"who": "ControlAI, A Narrow Path", "what": "would prohibit using AI systems to build or improve new ones, 2024.", "url": "https://www.narrowpath.co/phase-0"}, {"who": "Google DeepMind", "what": "added protocols for AI that could speed up AI research to destabilizing levels, 2025.", "url": "https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/strengthening-our-frontier-safety-framework/"}], "against": [{"who": "James Pethokoukis, AEI", "what": "argued a superintelligence ban would freeze progress and hand China an advantage, 2025.", "url": "https://www.aei.org/economics/ai-ban-backers-risk-freezing-progress/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 4 days behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/ai-rnd-limits.html",
  short: "AI agents can assist with AI research, but humans must direct and approve it.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers using AI systems to automate their own research.",
  clocks: { training: "cond", internal: "yes", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.05194, central: 0.13417, high: 0.26405 }, d_access: { low: 0.00107, central: 0.03353, high: 0.05408 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much faster AI-run research is making the labs, which they are only starting to measure.",
  requires: ["Require a human to direct and approve research experiments carried out by AI agents inside frontier labs.", "Prohibit fully autonomous AI research loops above a capability threshold.", "Report the share of research work done by AI systems."],
  baseline: "Both major frameworks already treat automated AI research as a danger sign. Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy v3.4 has an automated R&D threshold met if its models could fully substitute for its research scientists and engineers, or if AI progress doubled in speed. OpenAI's Preparedness Framework lists fully automated AI R&D as a Critical capability. Neither framework limits today's partial automation.",
  reasoning: `Existing human oversight is already in the baseline. Incremental checkpoints create service and handoff delays, magnified when reviewers are busy. Only affected task chains are charged. Fully autonomous work rises under an explicit first-year scenario rather than being assumed ubiquitous today.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 4 days behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 4 days, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `This is the policy most likely to get more expensive over time. If AI research automation is the main driver of progress in 2027 and beyond, keeping humans in every loop could halve the pace of the frontier. China's labs would face no such limit.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Anthropic, When AI builds itself", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/institute/recursive-self-improvement", note: "80% of merged code by Claude; engineers ship 8x as much code" },
    { title: "OpenAI, Research acceleration: the view inside OpenAI (Sept 2026)", url: "https://openai.com/index/research-acceleration-view-inside-openai/", note: "automated research intern goal met", secondary: "Engadget" },
    { title: "Anthropic Responsible Scaling Policy v3.4", url: "https://cdn.sanity.io/files/4zrzovbb/website/0bacdc8440ea96e62a8766d99ebe1d4eea6d5f3a.pdf", note: "automated R&D threshold" },
    { title: "OpenAI Preparedness Framework v2", url: "https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/18a02b5d-6b67-4cec-ab64-68cdfbddebcd/preparedness-framework-v2.pdf", note: "AI self-improvement: Critical means fully automated AI R&D" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "AI is doing more of the labs' research, but its boost is still modest." },

{ id: "safety-compute-share", section: "specials", name: "One-third of AI research budgets on safety",
  practice: "Frontier labs must spend at least a third of their AI research budget, including computing power, on safety work such as alignment, interpretability and testing, rather than on making models more capable.",
  why: "Moving spending up to a third of research budgets, from about 6% today, leaves the frontier with less compute for capabilities. Some safety work also helps capabilities, which softens the hit. A year in, that costs about a month of progress.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Bengio, Hinton and colleagues in Science", "what": "called for at least a third of AI R&D budgets to go to safety, 2024.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17688"}, {"who": "IDAIS-Oxford scientists", "what": "called for a minimum commitment of one third of AI R&D on safety, 2023.", "url": "https://idais.ai/dialogue/idais-oxford/"}, {"who": "IDAIS-Beijing scientists", "what": "repeated the call for at least a third, 2024.", "url": "https://idais.ai/dialogue/idais-beijing/"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 1 months behind where it would be; China loses about 3 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/safety-compute-share.html",
  short: "At least a third of AI R&D spending goes to safety, as leading researchers proposed in Science.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers' AI research budgets, including compute.",
  clocks: { training: "cond", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.47878, central: 1.1521, high: 1.86262 }, d_access: { low: 0.16859, central: 0.43986, high: 0.69692 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Medium-low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much labs already spend on safety, which none of them publish.",
  requires: ["Allocate at least one-third of AI research and development budgets to safety and ethical use.", "Count compute, not just salaries."],
  baseline: "Labs spend real money on safety research and testing but do not publish the share. Estimates in public discussion range from a few percent to perhaps a tenth of compute at the most safety-focused labs.",
  reasoning: `The one-third requirement covers R&D budgets. A one-week AI-R&D compute snapshot at one lab is informative but is not a measurement of industry-wide total-budget safety shares. Safety-capability overlap and a possible budget response reduce displacement. A permanent share change is a level/resource shock, not another identical annual delay.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 1 months behind its baseline path and China's is about 3 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 1 months, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `If labs spend far less than a tenth on safety today, the rule could take a quarter or more of their compute, and the cost would approach two months a year. Safety research also competes for the same scarce senior researchers.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Bengio, Hinton et al., Managing extreme AI risks amid rapid progress, Science (May 2024)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17688", note: "allocate at least one-third of their AI R&D budget to safety" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Training compute of frontier AI models grows by 4-5x per year", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/training-compute-of-frontier-ai-models-grows-by-4-5x-per-year" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Algorithmic progress in language models", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/algorithmic-progress-in-language-models", note: "60–95% of gains from compute and data" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Compute moved off capabilities costs about a month of progress a year in." },

{ id: "strict-liability", section: "mains", name: "Product liability for AI harms",
  practice: "AI systems are treated as products under the law. If one causes harm, the people hurt can sue the company that built it, the same way they could sue over a faulty car part.",
  why: "Lawsuits don't stop training, but they make launches more cautious and take senior time. The effect on the lead is under a day.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Sens. Durbin and Hawley", "what": "introduced the AI LEAD Act to let victims sue AI companies, 2025.", "url": "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/press/dem/releases/durbin-hawley-introduce-bill-allowing-victims-to-sue-ai-companies"}, {"who": "American Association for Justice, Encode AI and others", "what": "endorsed the AI LEAD Act, 2025.", "url": "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/press/dem/releases/durbin-hawley-introduce-bill-allowing-victims-to-sue-ai-companies"}, {"who": "Gabriel Weil, Touro Law", "what": "proposed strict liability for advanced AI as an abnormally dangerous activity, 2024.", "url": "https://www.private-law-theory.org/2024/01/28/gabriel-weil-tort-law-as-a-tool-for-mitigating-catastrophic-risk-from-artificial-intelligence/"}], "against": [{"who": "NetChoice", "what": "called treating AI systems as strict-liability products legally and practically misguided, 2026.", "url": "https://netchoice.org/netchoice-testimony-in-opposition-to-illinois-hb5044-the-chatbot-liability-act/"}, {"who": "Anjney Midha, a16z", "what": "called liability for downstream misuse misguided, 2024.", "url": "https://a16z.com/sb-1047-what-you-need-to-know-with-anjney-midha/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/strict-liability.html",
  short: "Victims can sue AI companies when their systems cause harm, under federal product liability law.",
  trigger: "Developers and deployers of AI systems.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00065, central: 0.00831, high: 0.01595 }, d_access: { low: 0.00311, central: 0.03852, high: 0.07401 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much caution the threat of lawsuits actually buys, and whether it spills over from product decisions into research.",
  requires: ["Classify AI systems as products for liability purposes.", "Create a federal cause of action for product liability claims when an AI system causes harm (the AI LEAD Act, S. 2937)."],
  baseline: "AI companies already face ordinary lawsuits, and many are pending. What they do not face is a clear federal rule that treats AI systems as products, which would make those suits easier to bring and win.",
  reasoning: `The financing channel multiplies exposed investment, an added risk premium, an investment response and productive redirection. A separate trust/demand offset allows net gains. Recent economics informs the channels; no medical-device coefficient is mechanically transplanted into frontier AI.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Open-ended liability can freeze a whole field. If juries treat any misuse of a general-purpose model as a product defect, labs could slow every release, stop publishing open models, and move research abroad. That could cost months, not weeks.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Durbin, Hawley introduce bill allowing victims to sue AI companies (Sept 29, 2025)", url: "https://www.durbin.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/durbin-hawley-introduce-bill-allowing-victims-to-sue-ai-companies" },
    { title: "S. 2937 bill status (govinfo)", url: "https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s2937.xml", note: "referred to Judiciary; no further action" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Lawsuits don't stop training, but they slow launches and eat senior time." },

{ id: "data-licensing", section: "specials", name: "Consent to train on copyrighted work",
  practice: "AI companies must get clear permission, usually a paid license, before training on copyrighted books, articles, art or personal data. Anyone whose work is used without consent can sue.",
  why: "Removing unlicensed data means training on less, or repeated, data. The model estimates about a month of lost progress a year in.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Sens. Hawley and Blumenthal", "what": "introduced a bill barring training on copyrighted works without consent, 2025.", "url": "https://www.hawley.senate.gov/hawley-blumenthal-unveil-bipartisan-bill-empowering-working-americans-to-sue-big-tech-ai-companies-for-stealing-creative-works/"}, {"who": "Authors Guild", "what": "welcomed the bill, 2025.", "url": "https://authorsguild.org/news/ag-welcomes-ai-accountability-and-personal-data-protection-act/"}, {"who": "News/Media Alliance", "what": "backs licensing of news content for AI training, though not a mandate, 2025.", "url": "https://www.newsmediaalliance.org/copyright-office-ai-report-press-release/"}], "against": [{"who": "Google and OpenAI", "what": "told the White House fair use is critical to AI development, 2025.", "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/13/google-calls-for-weakened-copyright-and-export-rules-in-ai-policy-proposal/"}, {"who": "Chamber of Progress", "what": "called the Hawley–Blumenthal bill a direct attack on fair use, 2025.", "url": "https://progresschamber.org/news/hawley-blumenthal-bill-undermines-innovation/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 4.5 weeks behind where it would be; China loses about 4 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/data-licensing.html",
  short: "No training on copyrighted works or personal data without clear, affirmative consent.",
  trigger: "Training of generative AI systems on copyrighted works or personal data.",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.56916, central: 0.98471, high: 1.57835 }, d_access: { low: 0.18448, central: 0.46452, high: 0.66055 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much training data labs could license quickly, and how much capability depends on the rest.",
  requires: ["Obtain clear, affirmative consent before using copyrighted works or personal data, including to train a generative AI system (AI Accountability and Personal Data Protection Act, S. 2367).", "Give individuals a right to sue for use without consent."],
  baseline: "Labs have begun licensing some data from publishers and paid to settle past claims. Anthropic's $1.5 billion settlement with authors covered about 482,000 pirated books and only past conduct. Most training data is still used without explicit consent.",
  reasoning: `Quality-adjusted unique data can be licensed, replaced, repeated or traded against parameters. The model reoptimizes the training recipe. Published saturation coefficients come from smaller models, so frontier transport and legally available substitutions remain important assumptions.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 4.5 weeks behind its baseline path and China's is about 4 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 4 weeks, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Chinese labs would not follow U.S. consent rules. If the rule removes a large share of high-quality text and code, the capability hit could persist for years, not a single training run.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "S. 2367 bill text (govinfo)", url: "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/BILLS-119s2367is/html/BILLS-119s2367is.htm", note: "covers training of a generative AI system" },
    { title: "Hawley and Blumenthal unveil bipartisan bill (July 2025)", url: "https://www.hawley.senate.gov/hawley-blumenthal-unveil-bipartisan-bill-empowering-working-americans-to-sue-big-tech-ai-companies-for-stealing-creative-works/" },
    { title: "Authors Alliance, Bartz v. Anthropic settlement gets preliminary approval", url: "https://www.authorsalliance.org/2025/09/28/bartz-v-anthropic-settlement-gets-preliminary-approval-key-takeaways/", note: "$1.5B for about 482,460 books" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Will we run out of data?", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/will-we-run-out-of-data-limits-of-llm-scaling-based-on-human-generated-data", note: "public text stock used up between 2026 and 2032" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Losing training data hits the next training run directly." },

{ id: "light-vetting", section: "sides", name: "Background checks for lab staff",
  practice: "Everyone who works at a frontier lab passes a standard background check, like the checks many banks and hospitals run. Nobody needs a security clearance.",
  why: "Standard checks barely slow hiring and make insider theft slightly harder. The two effects roughly cancel.",
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China falls under a day directly. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/light-vetting.html",
  short: "A standard background check for all lab employees, with no clearance-level vetting.",
  trigger: "All employees of frontier developers.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00175, high: 0.00311 }, d_access: { low: 0.0001, central: 0.00043, high: 0.00095 },
  d_china: { low: 0.00059, central: 0.00263, high: 0.00583 }, theft_cut: 0.027,
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much insider risk standard checks remove, which nobody has measured.",
  requires: ["Background checks for all employees (RAND security level 3).", "Risk-based screening scaled to access, rather than blanket exclusion (as proposed in Just Security, 2026)."],
  baseline: "Many labs already run some background screening. None publish how thorough it is.",
  reasoning: `This is ordinary background screening with work continuing where possible. It does not assume citizenship exclusion. Both the affected insider risk and productive days blocked are marginal to checks already conducted.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Even light checks can deter strong candidates who value privacy, and they can be applied unevenly to foreign-born researchers. If that pushes a few key people to labs abroad, the cost is larger than a few days.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "RAND, Securing AI Model Weights (2024)", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html", note: "background checks for all employees at SL3" },
    { title: "Just Security, Vetting foreign AI talent: security without exclusion (2026)", url: "https://www.justsecurity.org/145569/vetting-foreign-ai-talent-security-without-exclusion/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Cheap to run, small effect on spying. Roughly a wash." },

{ id: "personnel-security", section: "sides", name: "Clearance-level vetting for lab staff",
  practice: "Anyone with access to a lab's model weights or key research must pass vetting close to a government security clearance, with regular re-checks. This is the strict version; background checks alone are a separate item.",
  why: "Clearance-style vetting slows hiring and may lose some researchers, about 2 days of progress a year in. It also makes theft harder, which slows China by under a day. Net: about 2 days, and it could flip.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "RAND", "what": "recommends background checks for all staff and reliability programs for anyone with weight access, 2024.", "url": "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html"}, {"who": "Leopold Aschenbrenner", "what": "called for extreme personnel vetting and security clearances at labs, 2024.", "url": "https://situational-awareness.ai/lock-down-the-labs/"}, {"who": "Institute for Progress", "what": "recommended government screening for AI staff with access to sensitive systems, 2025.", "url": "https://ifp.org/a-sprint-toward-security-level-5/"}, {"who": "Joe Khawam and Tim Schnabel, Just Security", "what": "back risk-based vetting of foreign talent without blanket exclusion, 2026.", "url": "https://www.justsecurity.org/145569/vetting-foreign-ai-talent-security-without-exclusion/"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 2 days behind where it would be; China falls under a day directly; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/personnel-security.html",
  short: "Background checks for all staff; clearance-level vetting for anyone who touches the model weights.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers' staff with access to model weights or key research.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "cond", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.02455, central: 0.07308, high: 0.14162 }, d_access: { low: 0.00139, central: 0.02245, high: 0.03736 },
  d_china: { low: 0.00124, central: 0.02004, high: 0.03335 }, theft_cut: 0.205,
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How many researchers vetting would slow down or exclude, and how much espionage it would actually stop.",
  requires: ["Background checks for all employees (RAND security level 3).", "A personnel reliability program for staff with direct access to model weights (RAND security level 4)."],
  baseline: "Labs run some insider-threat programs, but none require government-style clearances. No 2025 or 2026 bill would mandate them. The Advanced AI Security Readiness Act asks the NSA to consider personnel vetting, and Senator Banks asked nine AI CEOs about their vetting in April 2026.",
  reasoning: `Sensitive roles, interim access, hiring flow, productive work retained during waiting and departures are modeled separately. A portion of lost U.S. capacity may join Chinese research. Government clearance processing time is a context anchor, not an observed private-lab delay.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 2 days behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 1 day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `If vetting drove even a tenth of top researchers out of U.S. labs, many would go to labs elsewhere, some in China. That could cost months, and it would hand talent to the country the rule is meant to protect against.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "RAND, Securing AI Model Weights (2024)", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html", note: "background checks at SL3; personnel reliability program at SL4" },
    { title: "Advanced AI Security Readiness Act, H.R. 3919", url: "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/BILLS-119hr3919ih/html/BILLS-119hr3919ih.htm", note: "NSA to consider personnel vetting" },
    { title: "Senator Banks letter on Chinese espionage in U.S. AI (April 2026)", url: "https://www.banks.senate.gov/news/press-releases/senator-banks-raises-concerns-over-chinese-espionage-targeting-u-s-artificial-intelligence-sector/" },
    { title: "MacroPolo Global AI Talent Tracker", url: "https://macropolo.org/interactive/digital-projects/the-global-ai-talent-tracker/" },
    { title: "House Select Committee testimony, Yusuf Mahmood (April 2026)", url: "https://docs.house.gov/meetings/ZS/ZS00/20260416/119165/HHRG-119-ZS00-Wstate-MahmoodY-20260416.pdf", note: "about 38% of top AI researchers at U.S. labs studied in China; the figure is attributed to MacroPolo but was not found in MacroPolo's own text", secondary: "House testimony" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "It slows hiring at home and spying from abroad." },

{ id: "superintelligence-ban", section: "specials", name: "Ban on superintelligence",
  practice: "No one may build AI that outperforms people across most areas. Under the Sanders–Casar bill, advanced AI development would also pause until a new federal Department of AI writes safety rules.",
  why: "While the pause holds, the U.S. frontier barely moves. A year in, America is about 5.5 months behind where it would have been, assuming China does not join.",
  how: "A pause with no end date, measured a year in: training stops while research continues, with no catch-up because the pause doesn't end. We estimate about 5.5 months behind, on the same logic as the modeled six-month pause.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Bernie Sanders and Rep. Greg Casar","what":"introduced the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act, September 2026.","url":"https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-casar-introduce-legislation-to-create-new-federal-agency-to-ban-artificial-superintelligence-pause-advanced-ai-development/"},{"who":"MIRI","what":"said humanity needs to back off from building superintelligence, 2026.","url":"https://intelligence.org/2026/09/23/miris-position-on-the-ban-artificial-superintelligence-act-of-2026/"},{"who":"Future of Life Institute","what":"organized the Statement on Superintelligence calling for a prohibition, 2025.","url":"https://futureoflife.org/press-release/prominent-scientists-faith-leaders-policymakers-and-artists-call-for-a-prohibition-on-superintelligence/"}],"against":[{"who":"Gary Marcus","what":"called a permanent, unilateral ban too broad but supports a temporary pause, 2026.","url":"https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/the-new-sanders-casar-ban-artificial"},{"who":"Caleb Max, National AI Association","what":"called the bill dead on arrival, 2026.","url":"https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/bernie-sanders-greg-casar-propose-ai-superintelligence-ban-20-year-jai-rcna599460"}]},
  short: "Ban AI that outperforms people across most areas, and pause advanced development until regulators set rules.",
  trigger: "Frontier AI development above compute thresholds set each year.",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "yes", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 3.61367, central: 5.58476, high: 7.88436 }, d_access: { low: 1.31406, central: 2.62812, high: 4.20499 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How long the pause would last and how much research would continue during it.",
  requires: ["Permanently prohibit superintelligent AI, defined as exceeding humans across most domains or able to disempower humanity.", "Pause advanced AI development until a new cabinet-level Department of AI sets safety rules.", "Use compute thresholds, updated yearly, to target recursive self-improvement; penalties up to 20 years in prison.", "Direct the U.S. to seek international agreements."],
  baseline: "No lab has stopped frontier development. Polls show broad public support for going slower: a 2025 Future of Life Institute poll found 64% of Americans say superhuman AI should not be built until proven safe, or never.",
  reasoning: `Senators Sanders and Representative Casar introduced the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act on September 23, 2026. It pairs a permanent ban on superintelligence with a pause on advanced development until a new Department of AI writes rules, which could take a year or more.
While the pause holds, large training runs stop. Research on existing models continues, and hardware and recipes keep improving, but with no end date there is no catch-up within the year. On the simulation model's logic for the six-month pause, we estimate the U.S. frontier ends the year about 5.5 months behind where it would have been, with a range of about 3.5 to 8 months.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `A pause with no end date may last far longer than a year, and a permanent ban on the most capable systems would end the U.S. frontier altogether. If China does not join, the lead would be gone within the first year.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Sanders and Casar introduce the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act (Sept 23, 2026)", url: "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-casar-introduce-legislation-to-create-new-federal-agency-to-ban-artificial-superintelligence-pause-advanced-ai-development/" },
    { title: "NBC News on the Sanders–Casar bill (Sept 23, 2026)", url: "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/bernie-sanders-greg-casar-propose-ai-superintelligence-ban-20-year-jai-rcna599460" },
    { title: "Statement on Superintelligence (Oct 2025)", url: "https://superintelligence-statement.org/" },
    { title: "Future of Life Institute poll on superhuman AI (2025)", url: "https://futureoflife.org/recent-news/americans-want-regulation-or-prohibition-of-superhuman-ai/", note: "64% say not until proven safe, or never" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "An open-ended pause on frontier development costs most of every year it lasts." },

{ id: "mutual-pause", section: "specials", name: "Verified six-month pause with China",
  practice: "The U.S. and China both stop training their most advanced models for six months, with inspections and chip tracking to check the other side is complying.",
  why: "Both countries stop, so most of the cost cancels. Verification tools aren't ready, so we assume China keeps some progress through cheating or hard-to-check work. Net: about 11 days a year in.",
  how: "Both countries pause six months. America loses about 51 days, as in the modeled six-month pause. We assume imperfect verification lets China keep about a fifth of its progress, so China loses about 40 days. Net: about 11 days.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"MIRI Technical Governance Team","what":"drafted an international agreement to prevent premature superintelligence, 2025.","url":"https://techgov.intelligence.org/research/an-international-agreement-to-prevent-the-premature-creation-of-artificial-superintelligence"},{"who":"IDAIS scientists including Hinton, Bengio, Andrew Yao and Stuart Russell","what":"called for global, verifiable red lines, 2025.","url":"https://idais.ai/dialogue/idais-shanghai/"},{"who":"Sen. Bernie Sanders and Rep. Greg Casar","what":"their bill directs the U.S. to seek international agreements, 2026.","url":"https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-casar-introduce-legislation-to-create-new-federal-agency-to-ban-artificial-superintelligence-pause-advanced-ai-development/"}],"against":[{"who":"President Trump","what":"said before the September 2026 summit that he wants to leave superintelligence exactly where it is.","url":"https://www.trumpstruth.org/statuses/41859"}]},
  short: "Both countries pause frontier training for six months, with verification.",
  trigger: "Frontier training runs in both the U.S. and China.",
  clocks: { training: "yes", internal: "yes", release: "yes" },
  d_us: { low: 1.1498, central: 1.67543, high: 2.29961 }, d_access: { low: 0.45335, central: 0.9067, high: 1.45072 }, d_china: { low: 0.65703, central: 1.31406, high: 1.67543 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether a pause can be verified. The key technologies have not been built or tested.",
  requires: ["Both countries halt training runs above an agreed threshold for six months.", "Verify compliance through chip tracking, monitored data centers and inspections."],
  baseline: "There is no U.S.–China agreement on frontier AI. In November 2024 Biden and Xi affirmed human control over nuclear launch decisions. In September 2026 the U.S. proposed an AI dialogue with a mechanism for notifying each other of serious incidents.",
  reasoning: `Every other pause on the menu assumes the U.S. acts alone. A verified mutual pause is what the 2023 open letter actually asked for, and it changes the math: if both sides stop, the gap does not move.
The catch is verification. Researchers writing on verification in 2025 found that many of the needed technologies have yet to be built or stress-tested. The simulation model prices a six-month U.S. pause at about 51 days behind a year later. We assume imperfect verification lets China keep about a fifth of that, so China loses about 40 days and the net is about 11 days. With good verification it could be close to zero; with poor verification it approaches the unilateral price.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `A verification regime would take years to build, so a pause agreed quickly would rely on trust. If China kept most of its progress, the U.S. would lose close to the full six months.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Baker, Kulp, Marks, Brundage, Heim, on verifying international AI agreements (2025)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15916", note: "many technologies have yet to be built or stress-tested" },
    { title: "MIRI Technical Governance Team, draft international agreement (2025)", url: "https://techgov.intelligence.org/research/an-international-agreement-to-prevent-the-premature-creation-of-artificial-superintelligence" },
    { title: "Al Jazeera, U.S.–China open high-level talks (Sept 20, 2026)", url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/9/20/us-china-open-high-level-talks-ahead-of-trump-xi-summit" },
    { title: "Dawn, Biden–Xi statement on nuclear launch decisions (Nov 2024)", url: "https://www.dawn.com/news/1873065" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "If both sides pause, most of the cost cancels. The question is cheating." },

{ id: "open-weight-limits", section: "mains", name: "Limits on releasing open-weight models",
  practice: "Developers may not publish the weights of models above a capability threshold. They can still offer those models through an app or API.",
  why: "Most U.S. frontier models are already closed, so the cost to U.S. progress is under a day. Keeping weights closed denies China free copies, which matters only if China depends on U.S. models; Chinese labs already release the largest open models.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's logic: under a day for the U.S. open ecosystem, since U.S. frontier labs are mostly closed. China loses about a day through its dependence on U.S. models at \"a little.\"",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Josh Hawley","what":"introduced a bill to decouple U.S. AI from China, including banning downloads of models like DeepSeek, 2025.","url":"https://www.hawley.senate.gov/hawley-introduces-legislation-to-decouple-american-ai-development-from-communist-china/"},{"who":"Henry Papadatos, SaferAI","what":"said dangerous capabilities should not be easily accessible by anyone anywhere, 2026.","url":"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/04/open-weight-ai-models-are-catching-up-to-the-frontier-the-safety-gap-remains/"}],"against":[{"who":"NTIA","what":"found evidence insufficient to warrant restrictions and recommended monitoring, 2024.","url":"https://www.ntia.gov/programs-and-initiatives/artificial-intelligence/open-model-weights-report"},{"who":"A 25-company coalition including Nvidia, Meta, Microsoft and Hugging Face","what":"wrote against open-weight restrictions, 2026.","url":"https://www.secureworld.io/industry-news/open-weight-ai-models"}]},
  short: "No publishing the weights of models above a capability threshold. API access stays allowed.",
  trigger: "Models above a capability threshold.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00329, central: 0.01643, high: 0.0657 }, d_access: { low: 0.0657, central: 0.13141, high: 0.21025 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much China gains from U.S. open weights when Chinese labs already publish the largest open models.",
  requires: ["Prohibit publishing the weights of models above a capability threshold.", "Allow access through hosted APIs and apps."],
  baseline: "No U.S. law restricts open weights. The 2025 AI Action Plan encourages open models and leaves the release decision to developers. Most U.S. frontier models are closed; Chinese labs release the largest open models.",
  reasoning: `NTIA's 2024 report found current evidence insufficient to justify restrictions and recommended monitoring. The 2025 AI Action Plan backs open models. In 2026, after Chinese labs released near-frontier open models, officials floated restrictions on Chinese open models; Anthropic said it has never advocated a ban.
Hugging Face reports that in almost every month of 2026, the largest open model from a Chinese lab was larger than any American one. That limits how much China gains from U.S. open weights. U.S. frontier labs mostly keep their best weights closed already, so we estimate under a day of lost U.S. progress. Denied open weights slow China by about a day at the "a little" setting, more if China depends heavily on U.S. models.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Open models are how most researchers, universities and startups work with frontier AI. Closing them could slow U.S. research broadly and cede the open ecosystem to Chinese models, which already lead downloads.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "NTIA, Open model weights report (July 2024)", url: "https://www.ntia.gov/programs-and-initiatives/artificial-intelligence/open-model-weights-report" },
    { title: "America's AI Action Plan (July 2025)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Americas-AI-Action-Plan.pdf", note: "supportive environment for open models" },
    { title: "Hugging Face, State of Open Models: Summer 2026", url: "https://huggingface.co/blog/state-of-open-models-summer-2026" },
    { title: "Lawfare, The knives are out for open-weight AI models (July 2026)", url: "https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/knives-are-out-for-open-weight-ai-models" },
    { title: "Anthropic, Position on open-weights models (July 2026)", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/news/position-open-weights-models" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "U.S. frontier labs are mostly closed already. China's open models lead downloads." },

{ id: "chip-sales", section: "offmenu", name: "Sell advanced AI chips to China",
  practice: "The U.S. licenses sales of advanced Nvidia and AMD chips, such as the H200, to Chinese companies, with the government taking a share of revenue.",
  why: "It costs U.S. labs nothing directly, but it feeds Chinese labs more compute. The model prices relaxing all chip controls at about four weeks; licensing these chips alone is about two weeks.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's export-control logic, which prices relaxing all controls at about four weeks: licensing H200-class chips alone is about two weeks, with deliveries so far far below the cap.",
  basis: "partial",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Jensen Huang, Nvidia","what":"called export controls a failure, 2025.","url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2025/05/21/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-slams-us-chip-restrictions-as-a-failure.html"},{"who":"David Sacks","what":"framed sales as selling lagging chips to take market share from Huawei, 2025.","url":"https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/biz/archives/2025/12/15/2003848878"},{"who":"Howard Lutnick, Commerce Secretary","what":"defended the licensing policy to the Senate, 2026.","url":"https://www.thestar.com.my/aseanplus/aseanplus-news/2026/04/26/china-bought-zero-h200-chips-as-of-today-says-lutnick-as-he-cites-delicate-balance-with-xi"}],"against":[{"who":"Sen. Elizabeth Warren","what":"said H200 sales risk turbocharging China's bid for technological and military dominance, 2025.","url":"https://www.banking.senate.gov/newsroom/minority/warren-statement-on-trump-administration-greenlighting-sale-of-h200-chips-to-china"},{"who":"Institute for Progress","what":"argued unrestricted H200 sales would substantially erode America's compute advantage, 2025.","url":"https://ifp.org/should-the-us-sell-hopper-chips-to-china/"}]},
  short: "License sales of advanced chips like Nvidia's H200 to China.",
  trigger: "Exports of advanced AI chips to China.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  d_china: { low: -1.31406, central: -0.49277, high: -0.09855 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How many chips China actually buys. Deliveries so far are a small fraction of what is licensed.",
  requires: ["License exports of H200-class chips to approved Chinese buyers, case by case.", "Cap China's shipments at 50% of U.S. shipments of the same chip; require U.S. testing and buyer security procedures.", "Government takes a share of revenue."],
  baseline: "In January 2026 the Commerce Department moved H200 licenses to case-by-case review with a 50% cap. China's government initially blocked purchases; by August 2026, ByteDance and Tencent had each received about 10,000 H200s.",
  reasoning: `The Institute for Progress estimates the U.S. lead in AI compute produced in 2026 is 21 to 49 times with no exports, falling to between 6.7 and 1.2 times with unrestricted H200 sales. IAPS estimates the licensed cap would raise China's total 2026 processing power by more than 250%, and CNAS estimates the cap allows about 890,000 H200s.
Converting compute to progress: a 3.5-fold increase in compute is roughly nine months of compute growth at fivefold a year, and compute drives most progress. At full use of the cap that would be several months of lead a year. The simulation model finds that relaxing all chip controls now would cost about four weeks of lead a year in, because compute takes time to turn into better models. Licensing H200-class chips alone, with deliveries so far far below the cap, we price at about two weeks, with a range of a few days to about six weeks.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `If China buys up to the cap, the compute gap could shrink from dozens of times to nearly even within a year, and the lead would erode faster than any safety rule on the menu could cost.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "BIS revises license review policy for chips to China (Jan 13, 2026)", url: "https://www.bis.gov/press-release/department-commerce-revises-license-review-policy-semiconductors-exported-china" },
    { title: "Institute for Progress, Should the US sell Hopper chips to China? (Dec 2025)", url: "https://ifp.org/should-the-us-sell-hopper-chips-to-china/" },
    { title: "IAPS, Assessing outcomes of H200 exports to China (Mar 2026)", url: "https://www.iaps.ai/research/assessing-outcomes-of-h200-exports-to-china", note: "more than 250% increase at the cap" },
    { title: "CNAS, Unpacking the H200 export policy (Jan 2026)", url: "https://www.cnas.org/publications/cnas-insights/cnas-insights-unpacking-the-h200-export-policy" },
    { title: "Engadget, China allows ByteDance and Tencent to import 10,000 H200s (Aug 2026)", url: "https://www.engadget.com/2239738/china-reportedly-allows-bytedance-tencent-import-10000-h200-chips/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "No cost to U.S. labs, but it feeds China's compute." },

{ id: "chip-tracking", section: "offmenu", name: "Location verification for AI chips",
  practice: "Export-controlled AI chips must carry a way to verify where they are, and exporters must report any sign that chips were diverted or tampered with.",
  why: "It costs U.S. labs nothing. Smuggled chips may be about a third of China's AI compute, so making smuggling harder slows China: about 5 days a year in.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's export-control logic: cutting smuggling, which may supply about a third of China's compute, is worth about 5 days a year in.",
  basis: "partial",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Tom Cotton and Rep. Bill Huizenga","what":"introduced the Chip Security Act, 2025.","url":"https://www.cotton.senate.gov/news/press-releases/cotton-huizenga-to-lutnick-stronger-export-controls-of-ai-chips-is-needed"},{"who":"Rep. John Moolenaar, House China Select Committee","what":"said America must defend its semiconductor advantage, 2026.","url":"https://chinaselectcommittee.house.gov/media/press-releases/house-committee-passes-chip-security-act"},{"who":"Americans for Responsible Innovation","what":"backed adding it to the defense bill, 2026.","url":"https://ari.us/senate-ndaa-takes-major-step-to-strengthen-ai-chip-export-controls/"}],"against":[{"who":"David Reber, Nvidia","what":"wrote that kill switches and backdoors create single points of failure, 2025.","url":"https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/no-backdoors-no-kill-switches-no-spyware"}]},
  short: "Require exported AI chips to verify their location, to stop smuggling to China.",
  trigger: "Export-controlled AI chips and their exporters.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  d_china: { low: 0.0, central: 0.16426, high: 0.49277 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much smuggling verification would stop, and how quickly smugglers would adapt.",
  requires: ["Within 180 days, require location verification on export-controlled AI chips (Chip Security Act).", "Require exporters to report diversion or tampering.", "Study further security mechanisms within a year."],
  baseline: "Not yet law. The Chip Security Act passed a House committee in March 2026 and was added to the Senate's defense bill in July 2026. Nvidia has released opt-in location tools.",
  reasoning: `CNAS estimated that 10,000 to several hundred thousand chips were smuggled to China in 2024. Epoch AI's 2026 analysis puts the total through 2025 at a median of 660,000 H100-equivalents, about a third of China's compute. The Justice Department indicted three people in March 2026 over about $2.5 billion of allegedly diverted servers.
If location verification cut smuggling by a third to a half, China would lose roughly a sixth of its compute. At fivefold yearly compute growth that is about a month of compute progress, and compute drives most progress, but the simulation model shows compute changes take time to show up in capability. We price about 5 days of Chinese delay a year in, with a range of zero to about two weeks.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Smugglers adapt: they rent chips abroad, route through third countries, or spoof location checks. If verification only shifts smuggling into cloud rental, the benefit could be near zero.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Chip Security Act bill text (2025)", url: "https://huizenga.house.gov/uploadedfiles/chip_security_act_5.13.25_final.pdf" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Chip smuggling (Apr 2026)", url: "https://epoch.ai/publications/chip-smuggling", note: "median 660,000 H100-equivalents smuggled through 2025" },
    { title: "CNAS, Countering AI chip smuggling (June 2025)", url: "https://www.cnas.org/publications/reports/countering-ai-chip-smuggling-has-become-a-national-security-priority" },
    { title: "FDD on the Super Micro-linked smuggling indictment (Mar 2026)", url: "https://www.fdd.org/analysis/2026/03/20/exposure-of-major-chinese-linked-chip-smuggling-operations-shows-limits-of-industry-self-policing/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Free for U.S. labs, and smuggled chips are a big share of China's compute." },

{ id: "preemption", section: "speedups", name: "Federal preemption of state AI laws",
  practice: "Congress bars states from writing their own rules for frontier AI developers, leaving one national rulebook. The White House's 2026 framework asks for this with no new federal regulator.",
  why: "State frontier laws are mostly paperwork labs already do, and the model prices them at under a day. So removing them buys back under a day. Preemption matters more for other state laws on how AI is used, which barely touch the frontier.",
  how: "Our estimate: removes the state frontier laws, which the model prices at under a day combined.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Ted Cruz","what":"called for preventing a patchwork of burdensome AI regulation, 2025.","url":"https://www.commerce.senate.gov/2025/9/sen-cruz-ai-policy-should-harness-the-power-of-american-ingenuity"},{"who":"Reps. Jay Obernolte and Lori Trahan","what":"included preemption in the FRONTIER Act, 2026.","url":"https://obernolte.house.gov/media/press-releases/obernolte-trahan-introduce-bipartisan-frontier-act-strengthen-oversight"},{"who":"The White House","what":"asked Congress to preempt unduly burdensome state AI laws, March 2026.","url":"https://www.morganlewis.com/pubs/2026/03/white-house-ai-framework-puts-federal-preemption-at-the-center-of-the-debate"}],"against":[{"who":"Sens. Maria Cantwell and Marsha Blackburn","what":"led the 99–1 vote stripping the state-law moratorium, 2025.","url":"https://www.commerce.senate.gov/press/dem/release/senate-strikes-ai-moratorium-from-budget-reconciliation-bill-in-overwhelming-99-1-vote-2025-7/"},{"who":"Brad Carson, Americans for Responsible Innovation","what":"said a preemption draft turns the current floor into a federal ceiling, 2026.","url":"https://rollcall.com/2026/06/04/bipartisan-ai-draft-proposes-three-year-preemption-of-state-laws/"}]},
  short: "One national rulebook: states may not regulate frontier AI development.",
  trigger: "State laws that regulate AI developers.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -0.01643, central: -0.00329, high: -0.00033 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much the broader patchwork of state AI laws, beyond frontier laws, actually slows frontier labs. No study has measured it.",
  requires: ["Prohibit states from adopting or enforcing laws that impose new obligations on AI developers.", "Leave generally applicable laws and protections for minors in place, as most proposals do."],
  baseline: "No preemption law has passed. The Senate stripped a 10-year moratorium 99–1 in July 2025. An executive order in December 2025 created a Justice Department task force to challenge state laws; it had filed no lawsuits by September 2026. A House draft would preempt state laws on development for three years.",
  reasoning: `The frontier-specific state laws on this menu, California's SB 53 and New York's RAISE Act, each cost under a day in the simulation model, and they largely overlap. Preempting them buys back under a day.
The broader patchwork is larger. Thirty-eight states adopted around 100 AI measures in 2025, according to NCSL. But most regulate how AI is used by deployers, in hiring, insurance or chatbots, and rarely touch how frontier labs train models. We found no study measuring their effect on frontier progress. We price the frontier effect at under a day, with a range up to half a day.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Supporters argue the real cost of a patchwork is not paperwork but uncertainty: fifty different liability rules could make labs slower and more cautious across every product. If that is right, preemption is worth more than a week.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Senate strikes AI moratorium 99–1 (July 1, 2025)", url: "https://www.commerce.senate.gov/press/dem/release/senate-strikes-ai-moratorium-from-budget-reconciliation-bill-in-overwhelming-99-1-vote-2025-7/" },
    { title: "Executive Order 14365 (Dec 11, 2025)", url: "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-14365-ensuring-national-policy-framework-for-artificial-intelligence" },
    { title: "FRONTIER Act text", url: "https://trahan.house.gov/uploadedfiles/oberno_079_xml_-_the_frontier_act_-_final_text.pdf", note: "preemption clause" },
    { title: "Roll Call, three-year preemption draft (June 2026)", url: "https://rollcall.com/2026/06/04/bipartisan-ai-draft-proposes-three-year-preemption-of-state-laws/" },
    { title: "NCSL, Artificial intelligence 2025 legislation", url: "https://www.ncsl.org/technology-and-communication/artificial-intelligence-2025-legislation", note: "38 states adopted around 100 measures" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "State frontier laws are mostly paperwork, so removing them buys back under a day." },

{ id: "visa-restrictions", section: "speedups", name: "$100,000 fee on new H-1B visas",
  practice: "Employers pay $100,000 for each new H-1B petition for a skilled foreign worker. A 2025 proclamation set the fee; courts have blocked it, and a similar fee has been proposed by regulation.",
  why: "Frontier labs can afford the fee. The effect is a thinner pipeline of students and researchers, which builds slowly: about 2 days a year in.",
  how: "Our estimate: about 2 days a year in, mostly from a thinner pipeline of students and researchers. Labs can pay the fee.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Howard Lutnick, Commerce Secretary","what":"said if you're going to train people, train Americans, 2025.","url":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-signs-proclamation-that-adds-100k-annual-fee-to-h-1b-visas-for-high-skilled-workers"},{"who":"Rep. Riley Moore","what":"introduced the Stop CCP VISAs Act, 2025.","url":"https://rileymoore.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-moore-introduces-stop-ccp-visas-act"}],"against":[{"who":"U.S. Chamber of Commerce","what":"sued to block the fee, 2025.","url":"https://www.uschamber.com/cases/labor-and-employment/chamber-of-commerce-v-dhs"},{"who":"Institute for Progress","what":"found most top U.S. AI companies were founded by immigrants, 2025.","url":"https://ifp.org/most-of-americas-top-ai-companies-were-founded-by-immigrants/"}]},
  short: "A $100,000 fee on each new H-1B petition for skilled foreign workers.",
  trigger: "New H-1B petitions.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00986, central: 0.0657, high: 0.26281 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much a fee changes who comes to the U.S. to study and work in AI. The fee is currently blocked in court.",
  requires: ["Pay $100,000 with each new H-1B petition (2025 proclamation); renewals exempt.", "A proposed 2026 regulation would set a $103,265 fee on cap-subject petitions, exempting universities and nonprofit research groups."],
  baseline: "The fee took effect in September 2025, was vacated by a federal court in June 2026, and remains unenforceable. The proclamation was extended to September 2027. New international student enrollment fell 17% in fall 2025.",
  reasoning: `CSET found in 2019 that two-thirds of graduate students in AI-related programs were international and that most stay in the U.S. after graduating. The Institute for Progress found that 25 of the 42 U.S. companies on the Forbes AI 50 had an immigrant founder, and most of those founders first came on student visas.
A $100,000 fee is small next to a frontier researcher's pay, so large labs would mostly absorb it. The effect falls on startups, universities and the students who become tomorrow's researchers. The pipeline effect builds slowly, so a year in we estimate about 2 days of lost progress, with a range of under a day to about a week.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Talent compounds. If the U.S. becomes a less reliable destination, top students go elsewhere, and some go to China. The cost could grow year after year and exceed a month.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Proclamation restricting entry of certain nonimmigrant workers (Sept 19, 2025)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/09/restriction-on-entry-of-certain-nonimmigrant-workers/" },
    { title: "CUPA-HR, federal court vacates H-1B fee (June 2026)", url: "https://www.cupahr.org/resource/federal-court-vacates-h-1b-visa-fee-policy/" },
    { title: "DiRaimondo & Schroeder, proclamation extended but fee blocked (Sept 2026)", url: "https://www.diraimondoschroeder.com/updates/2026/9/21/trump-administration-extends-100000-h-1b-proclamation-through-september-2027-but-fee-remains-blocked-by-court-order" },
    { title: "CSET, Keeping top AI talent in the United States (2019)", url: "https://cset.georgetown.edu/publication/keeping-top-ai-talent-in-the-united-states/" },
    { title: "IIE Open Doors 2025", url: "https://www.iie.org/news/open-doors-2025-press-release/", note: "new international enrollment down 17%" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Labs can pay the fee. The pipeline of students is what thins." },

{ id: "fast-visas", section: "speedups", name: "Fast-track visas for AI researchers",
  practice: "Make it quick and easy for top AI researchers to get U.S. visas and green cards, for example by exempting U.S. STEM PhDs from green-card caps and expanding O-1 visas.",
  why: "Most AI talent in the U.S. arrives through universities and visas. Removing delays and caps adds researchers at the margin: about 2 days of progress a year in.",
  how: "Our estimate, the mirror image of the H-1B fee: about 2 days gained a year in.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Institute for Progress","what":"proposed expanding O-1A visas, recapturing unused green cards and more, 2025.","url":"https://ifp.org/strengthening-americas-ai-workforce/"},{"who":"Sens. Dick Durbin, Mike Rounds and Angus King","what":"backed the Keep STEM Talent Act, 2025.","url":"https://www.king.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/king-cosponsors-bipartisan-legislation-to-allow-international-students-remain-in-the-us"},{"who":"CSET","what":"recommended policies to keep top AI talent in the U.S., 2019.","url":"https://cset.georgetown.edu/publication/keeping-top-ai-talent-in-the-united-states/"}],"against":[]},
  short: "Faster visas and green cards for top AI researchers.",
  trigger: "AI researchers and STEM graduates seeking U.S. visas.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -0.26281, central: -0.0657, high: -0.00986 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How many additional top researchers faster visas would actually bring or keep.",
  requires: ["Exempt U.S. STEM master's and PhD graduates from green-card caps (Keep STEM Talent Act).", "Expand and speed O-1A visas and recapture unused green cards (Institute for Progress proposals)."],
  baseline: "No AI-specific visa exists. The 2025 AI Action Plan contains no immigration measures. The Keep STEM Talent Act was introduced in 2025 without further action we could verify.",
  reasoning: `CSET found most international AI PhDs take U.S. jobs and stay at least five years, so the main effect of fast-track visas is at the margin: more arrivals, fewer departures, and less time lost to visa limbo. The Institute for Progress argues international talent is necessary for U.S. AI leadership.
We price the mirror image of the H-1B fee: about 2 days gained a year in, ranging from under a day to about a week.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Frontier labs already get the talent they want through existing visas; faster green cards mostly help people who would have come anyway. The gain could be close to zero.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Institute for Progress, Strengthening America's AI workforce (2025)", url: "https://ifp.org/strengthening-americas-ai-workforce/" },
    { title: "Sen. King on the Keep STEM Talent Act (2025)", url: "https://www.king.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/king-cosponsors-bipartisan-legislation-to-allow-international-students-remain-in-the-us" },
    { title: "CSET, Keeping top AI talent in the United States (2019)", url: "https://cset.georgetown.edu/publication/keeping-top-ai-talent-in-the-united-states/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "The mirror image of the H-1B fee." },

{ id: "dc-permitting", section: "speedups", name: "Fast permits and power for AI data centers",
  practice: "Speed up environmental reviews and grid connections for large AI data centers and the power plants that feed them, as a 2025 executive order and pending permitting bills aim to do.",
  why: "Power is now a main limit on how fast labs add compute, and new plants wait years to connect to the grid. Faster permits get some compute online sooner: about 10 days of progress a year in.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's compute logic: if faster permits bring part of new compute online a couple of months sooner, the frontier gains about 10 days a year in.",
  basis: "partial",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Anthropic","what":"urged faster permitting and grid buildout for AI, 2025.","url":"https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/0dc382a2086f6a054eeb17e8a531bd9625b8e6e5.pdf"},{"who":"Institute for Progress","what":"proposed faster, flexible grid interconnection for AI, 2026.","url":"https://ifp.org/interconnection-for-ai/"}],"against":[{"who":"Earthjustice Action","what":"said weakening bedrock environmental laws is not the solution, 2025.","url":"https://earthjusticeaction.org/news/earthjustice-action-statement-on-speed-act/"}]},
  short: "Faster environmental reviews and grid connections for AI data centers and power plants.",
  trigger: "Data centers over 100 megawatts and the generation that serves them.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -1.31406, central: -0.32852, high: -0.03285 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much permitting, as opposed to chips, turbines and transformers, is the binding limit on new compute.",
  requires: ["Create faster environmental review paths and federal-land siting for data centers over 100 megawatts (July 2025 executive order).", "Speed grid interconnection for large loads and new generation.", "Pass permitting reform such as the SPEED Act."],
  baseline: "The July 2025 order is in effect. FERC ordered grid operators in June 2026 to address large-load interconnection. The SPEED Act passed the House in December 2025; Senate talks were ongoing in September 2026.",
  reasoning: `Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory finds that the median time from an interconnection request to operation exceeded five years for projects built in 2025. Anthropic estimates U.S. AI will need at least 50 gigawatts by 2028 and contrasts China's months-long permitting with U.S. reviews that take years. The Institute for Progress says flexible connections can bring a 500 megawatt data center online three to five years sooner.
In the simulation model, compute is one input among several and labs partly substitute when it is scarce. If faster permitting brings part of new compute online a couple of months sooner, we estimate the frontier ends the year about 10 days ahead. The range runs from a day to about six weeks because other bottlenecks, like chips and transformers, may bind first.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `For a lower gain: labs build where power is already available, and chips, not permits, set the pace. For a higher one: if power is the binding constraint by 2027, permitting could be worth several months a year.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Executive order on accelerating federal permitting of data center infrastructure (July 2025)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/07/accelerating-federal-permitting-of-data-center-infrastructure/" },
    { title: "Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Queued Up 2026", url: "https://eta-publications.lbl.gov/publications/queued-2026-edition-characteristics", note: "median over five years to connect" },
    { title: "Anthropic, Build AI in America (2025)", url: "https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/0dc382a2086f6a054eeb17e8a531bd9625b8e6e5.pdf" },
    { title: "Institute for Progress, Interconnection for AI (2026)", url: "https://ifp.org/interconnection-for-ai/" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Power demands of frontier AI training (2025)", url: "https://epoch.ai/blog/power-demands-of-frontier-ai-training" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Power is a real bottleneck. Faster permits mean compute sooner." },

{ id: "public-compute", section: "speedups", name: "Public AI research compute",
  practice: "Government provides computing power for university and public-interest AI research, through the National AI Research Resource and California's CalCompute.",
  why: "It broadens access to AI research, but its scale is tiny next to frontier labs. Under a day.",
  how: "Our estimate: the public resource is tiny next to frontier labs, under a day.",
  basis: "partial",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sens. Martin Heinrich, Todd Young, Mike Rounds and Cory Booker","what":"reintroduced the CREATE AI Act to make the research resource permanent, 2026.","url":"https://www.heinrich.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/heinrich-young-rounds-booker-reintroduce-bipartisan-legislation-to-expand-access-to-artificial-intelligence-research"},{"who":"Sen. Scott Wiener","what":"created CalCompute in SB 53, 2025.","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53"}],"against":[]},
  short: "Government computing power for university and public AI research.",
  trigger: "Researchers outside frontier labs.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -0.03285, central: -0.00657, high: 0.0 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "Whether public research feeds frontier progress through ideas rather than compute.",
  requires: ["Fund the National AI Research Resource permanently (CREATE AI Act).", "Build California's public compute cluster, CalCompute, if funded."],
  baseline: "The NSF pilot supported over 800 projects; an operations center was set up in September 2026. The CREATE AI Act passed a House committee 29–0 in June 2026. CalCompute operates only if the state funds it.",
  reasoning: `CSET estimated the pilot's compute at about 3.77 exaFLOPS, roughly equal to 5,000 H100 chips, while companies like Meta and Microsoft each bought over 150,000 H100s. Public compute supports academic research and training, which matters for the long-run talent pipeline, but it does not move the frontier's own training runs.
We estimate a gain of under a day, with a range of zero to about a day.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Academic research produced many of the ideas frontier labs now use. If public compute seeds the next big algorithmic advance, its value could be far larger than its chip count suggests.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "NSF establishes the NAIRR Operations Center (Sept 2026)", url: "https://www.nsf.gov/cise/updates/nsf-establishes-operations-center-national-artificial" },
    { title: "CSET, The NAIRR pilot: estimating compute (2024)", url: "https://cset.georgetown.edu/article/the-nairr-pilot-estimating-compute/" },
    { title: "Heinrich, Young, Rounds, Booker reintroduce the CREATE AI Act (2026)", url: "https://www.heinrich.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/heinrich-young-rounds-booker-reintroduce-bipartisan-legislation-to-expand-access-to-artificial-intelligence-research" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Useful for universities, tiny next to frontier labs." },

{ id: "manhattan-project", section: "speedups", name: "A government \"Manhattan Project\" for AI",
  practice: "The federal government funds and leads a crash program to build the most advanced AI, as a 2024 congressional commission recommended. The 2025 Genesis Mission is a much smaller, science-focused version.",
  why: "Government money and priority access to power could speed the frontier, but private spending is already huge. A program adding about a tenth more compute would gain about 12 days a year in; a much larger one, more.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's compute logic: a program adding roughly a tenth more compute would gain about 12 days a year in. Depends entirely on size.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission","what":"recommended a Manhattan Project-like program to race to AGI, 2024.","url":"https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2024-11/2024_Comprehensive_List_of_Recommendations.pdf"},{"who":"Energy Secretary Chris Wright","what":"launched the Genesis Mission, 2025.","url":"https://www.energy.gov/articles/energy-department-launches-genesis-mission-transform-american-science-and-innovation"}],"against":[{"who":"Max Tegmark, Future of Life Institute","what":"said an AGI race is a suicide race, 2024.","url":"https://futureoflife.org/statement/agi-manhattan-project-max-tegmark/"}]},
  short: "A federally funded crash program to build the most advanced AI.",
  trigger: "Federal AI research and compute.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -1.97109, central: -0.39422, high: -0.0657 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  market_price: true,
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "The program's size, and whether China would respond by racing harder.",
  requires: ["Establish and fund a Manhattan Project-like program to acquire advanced AI capability (USCC 2024 recommendation).", "The 2025 Genesis Mission instead builds a Department of Energy AI platform for science, with about $5 billion in announced commitments."],
  baseline: "No crash program exists. The Genesis Mission launched in November 2025 and announced over $5 billion in commitments across 278 projects in July 2026.",
  reasoning: `The U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission's first 2024 recommendation was a Manhattan Project-like program racing to AGI. The 2025 Genesis Mission borrows the Manhattan Project comparison but funds science applications at a far smaller scale.
Private frontier spending already runs to hundreds of billions of dollars a year. A government program adds on top, and could help most by clearing power and chip bottlenecks. Using the simulation model's link between compute and progress, a program adding about a tenth more frontier compute would put the U.S. about 12 days ahead a year in. The range runs from a couple of days for a Genesis-sized program to about two months for a large crash effort. We do not model China's likely response, which could offset some of the gain.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `A crash program could provoke China to race harder and abandon any safety cooperation, erasing the gain. Critics also argue it would concentrate power and sideline safety.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "USCC 2024 comprehensive list of recommendations", url: "https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2024-11/2024_Comprehensive_List_of_Recommendations.pdf" },
    { title: "Launching the Genesis Mission (Nov 24, 2025)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/11/launching-the-genesis-mission/" },
    { title: "White House Genesis Mission update (July 2026)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/releases/2026/07/45502/", note: "more than $5 billion in commitments" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Big money could speed things up, but private spending is already huge." },

{ id: "dna-screening", section: "starters", name: "DNA synthesis screening",
  practice: "Companies that make synthetic DNA must screen orders and customers so no one can buy the genetic material for a dangerous pathogen, including designs an AI helped create.",
  why: "The rule falls on DNA synthesis companies, not AI developers. It closes one route by which AI could help someone build a bioweapon, at no cost to America's AI lead.",
  how: "No cost to AI developers: the burden falls on DNA synthesis providers.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sens. Tom Cotton and Amy Klobuchar","what":"introduced a bill requiring providers to screen orders and verify customers, 2026.","url":"https://www.cotton.senate.gov/news/press-releases/cotton-klobuchar-introduce-bill-to-establish-federal-biotech-security-framework"},{"who":"Americans for Responsible Innovation","what":"backed the Cotton–Klobuchar bill to address AI-era biosecurity risks, 2026.","url":"https://ari.us/cotton-klobuchar-intro-biosecurity-modernization-and-innovation-act-to-address-ai-era-biosecurity-risks/"},{"who":"The White House AI Action Plan","what":"recommended enforced screening for federally funded institutions, 2025.","url":"https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Americas-AI-Action-Plan.pdf"}],"against":[]},
  short: "Screen synthetic DNA orders and customers to block bioweapon ingredients.",
  trigger: "Providers of synthetic DNA and RNA.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "High", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "None for the lead. Whether screening works against AI-designed sequences is a separate question.",
  requires: ["Require DNA synthesis providers to screen orders against sequences of concern and verify customers (Biosecurity Modernization and Innovation Act, S.3741).", "Require federally funded institutions to buy only from screening providers (2024 framework; AI Action Plan)."],
  baseline: "A 2024 framework made screening a condition of federal research funding; a 2025 executive order called for it to be revised or replaced. A Cotton–Klobuchar bill introduced in January 2026 is in committee.",
  reasoning: `This is a safety rule that never touches an AI lab. The cost falls on DNA synthesis providers, several of which, like Twist and IDT, endorse the Cotton–Klobuchar bill. It addresses the risk that AI lowers the barrier to building biological weapons, which a 2025 Science paper illustrated by showing AI-redesigned toxins slipping past screening software.
It costs America's AI lead nothing.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `There is no plausible case that DNA screening slows frontier AI. Critics instead argue that screening alone will not hold as a choke point and that safeguards must also go into the AI models.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "ASPR, framework for nucleic acid synthesis screening", url: "https://www.aspr.gov/readiness-response/medical-countermeasures-biodefense/s3/synthetic-nucleic-acid-screening/ostp-framework-nucleic-acid-synthesis-screening" },
    { title: "Biosecurity Modernization and Innovation Act, S.3741 (Jan 2026)", url: "https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/s3741/BILLS-119s3741is.htm" },
    { title: "America's AI Action Plan (July 2025)", url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Americas-AI-Action-Plan.pdf", note: "enforced screening for federally funded institutions" },
    { title: "MIT Technology Review on AI-designed toxins evading screening (Oct 2025)", url: "https://www.technologyreview.com/2025/10/02/1124767/microsoft-says-ai-can-create-zero-day-threats-in-biology/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "The burden falls on DNA makers, not AI labs." },

{ id: "watermarking", section: "starters", name: "Watermarks on AI-generated content",
  practice: "AI companies must embed hidden, machine-readable labels in the images, video and audio their models generate, offer a visible label, and provide a free tool to check whether content is AI-made.",
  why: "Labs that generate media add labels using standards they already help build. The work displaces under a day of research and holds up no model.",
  how: "Our estimate: engineering work to label outputs displaces under a day of research.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Josh Becker","what":"wrote California's AI Transparency Act, 2024.","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billStatusClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB1000"},{"who":"Sens. Maria Cantwell, Marsha Blackburn and Martin Heinrich","what":"reintroduced the COPIED Act on content provenance, 2025.","url":"https://www.commerce.senate.gov/press/dem/release/cantwell-blackburn-heinrich-reintroduce-bipartisan-bill-to-increase-transparency-combat-ai-deepfakes-put-journalists-artists-songwriters-back-in-control-of-their-content-2025-4/"}],"against":[{"who":"TechNet and CCIA","what":"said provenance technologies remain emergent and complex, 2025.","url":"https://apcp.assembly.ca.gov/system/files/2025-04/ab-853-wicks-apcp-analysis.pdf"},{"who":"Chamber of Progress","what":"asked the governor to veto the AI Transparency Act, 2024.","url":"https://progresschamber.org/resources/letter-to-ca-governor-veto-problematic-ai-transparency-bill-sb-942/"}]},
  short: "Hidden labels on AI-generated images, video and audio, plus a free detection tool.",
  trigger: "Generative AI providers with large user bases; in the EU, all general-purpose model providers.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00164, central: 0.00657, high: 0.01971 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much engineering reliable watermarking takes, especially for text in the EU.",
  requires: ["Embed hidden, machine-readable provenance in AI-generated image, video and audio (California AI Transparency Act, operative August 2, 2026).", "Offer a visible label and a free public detection tool.", "EU AI Act Article 50: mark AI outputs in a machine-readable format."],
  baseline: "Major labs sit on the steering committee of C2PA, the industry provenance standard, and many already attach Content Credentials to generated images.",
  reasoning: `California's AI Transparency Act became operative on August 2, 2026, and the EU's transparency rules applied from the same date, with a grace period to December 2026 for existing systems. Both lean on standards like C2PA that the largest labs helped build.
The work is engineering and compliance, not training. It displaces under a day of research.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Robust watermarking of text is an unsolved problem, and the EU rule covers text. If regulators demand watermarks that survive editing, labs could spend far more engineering time than a few days.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Morgan Lewis, New California AI disclosure rules become operative (Aug 2026)", url: "https://www.morganlewis.com/pubs/2026/08/new-california-ai-disclosure-rules-become-operative" },
    { title: "European Commission, Code of Practice on AI-generated content", url: "https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/code-practice-ai-generated-content" },
    { title: "C2PA", url: "https://c2pa.org/" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Labels on outputs. Engineering work, not a training delay." },

{ id: "chatbot-safety", section: "starters", name: "Safety rules for AI companion chatbots",
  practice: "Chatbots must tell users they are talking to an AI, respond to signs of suicide or self-harm with crisis resources, and for minors, remind them to take breaks and block sexual content.",
  why: "These rules shape consumer products, not training. The product and compliance work displaces under a day of research.",
  how: "Our estimate: product safeguards displace under a day of research.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Steve Padilla","what":"wrote California's first companion-chatbot safeguards law, 2025.","url":"https://sd18.senate.ca.gov/news/first-nation-ai-chatbot-safeguards-signed-law"},{"who":"Jim Steyer, Common Sense Media","what":"backed California's 2026 child-safety chatbot law, 2026.","url":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/press-releases/governor-newsom-signs-adams-law-setting-first-real-safety-standard-for-kids-and-chatbots"},{"who":"Sens. Josh Hawley and Richard Blumenthal","what":"their GUARD Act passed Senate Judiciary unanimously, 2026.","url":"https://www.hawley.senate.gov/senator-hawleys-guard-act-to-protect-kids-from-ai-chatbots-passes-committee-unanimously/"}],"against":[{"who":"Electronic Frontier Foundation","what":"said the GUARD Act creates serious problems for privacy, speech and parental choice, 2026.","url":"https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/05/congress-narrowed-guard-act-serious-problems-remain"}]},
  short: "AI disclosure, crisis protocols and protections for minors in companion chatbots.",
  trigger: "Operators of companion chatbots; possibly general-purpose assistants.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00164, central: 0.00657, high: 0.02628 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether general-purpose assistants like ChatGPT and Claude count as companion chatbots, and how heavy age verification would be under the GUARD Act.",
  requires: ["Disclose that the chatbot is an AI; maintain a suicide and self-harm protocol with crisis referrals (California SB 243, effective January 1, 2026).", "For known minors: break reminders every three hours and no sexual content.", "Annual risk assessments and audits for child safety (California SB 1119, effective July 2027)."],
  baseline: "California, New York and at least 13 other states enacted chatbot safety laws in 2025 and 2026. The federal GUARD Act passed committee in April 2026. OpenAI publicly backed California's 2026 law.",
  reasoning: `These laws target consumer chatbot products. They require disclosures, crisis protocols and protections for minors, all of which are product features rather than training changes. The Future of Privacy Forum notes uncertainty about whether general-purpose assistants are covered; OpenAI's support for California's 2026 law suggests it expects to be.
The product and compliance work displaces under a day of research.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Strict age verification, as in the GUARD Act, could require identity checks for every user of a general-purpose assistant. That is a large product change, though still not a delay to training.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "California SB 243 text", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB243" },
    { title: "Troutman, Analyzing the new AI companion chatbot laws (Jan 2026)", url: "https://www.troutmanprivacy.com/2026/01/analyzing-the-new-ai-companion-chatbot-laws/" },
    { title: "Governor Newsom signs child-safety chatbot laws (Sept 2026)", url: "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/10/governor-newsom-signs-the-strongest-child-safety-chatbot-and-social-media-laws-in-the-nation/" },
    { title: "Transparency Coalition, 14 new state chatbot laws in 2026", url: "https://www.transparencycoalition.ai/news/watershed-year-for-chatbot-safety-measures-14-new-state-laws-enacted-so-far-in-2026" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Product rules for chatbots, not training rules." },

{ id: "algo-discrimination", section: "starters", name: "Rules against algorithmic discrimination",
  practice: "When AI is used for consequential decisions like hiring, lending or housing, developers must document what their systems are for and their limits, and the companies using them must notify people and allow appeals.",
  why: "These laws mainly fall on businesses using AI to make decisions. For frontier developers, Colorado's current law asks only for documentation: under a day.",
  how: "Our estimate: documentation work displaces under a day of research.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Colorado Sen. Robert Rodriguez","what":"sponsored Colorado's original AI Act, 2024.","url":"https://tsscolorado.com/with-reservations-polis-signs-landmark-ai-regulation-bill/"}],"against":[{"who":"Gov. Jared Polis","what":"warned a state patchwork can tamper innovation, 2024.","url":"https://tsscolorado.com/with-reservations-polis-signs-landmark-ai-regulation-bill/"},{"who":"xAI and the Justice Department","what":"challenged Colorado's law in court, 2026.","url":"https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en/knowledge/publications/de3ad9de/xai-sues-doj-intervenes-enforcement-of-colorado-ai-act-suspended"}]},
  short: "Documentation, notice and appeals when AI makes consequential decisions about people.",
  trigger: "AI used in consequential decisions such as jobs, lending and housing.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00066, central: 0.00329, high: 0.01314 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "Medium", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether other states adopt heavier duties of care than Colorado's current documentation rule.",
  requires: ["Developers give deployers documentation on intended uses, training-data categories and known limitations, and keep records for three years (Colorado SB26-189, effective January 1, 2027).", "Deployers give notice, explain adverse decisions within 30 days and offer human review."],
  baseline: "Colorado's 2024 AI Act was delayed, challenged by xAI with Justice Department support, and replaced in May 2026 by a narrower law focused on documentation and notice.",
  reasoning: `This is the kind of law critics of the state patchwork most often cite. Colorado's replacement law puts most duties on deployers, the employers, lenders and landlords using AI, and asks developers mainly for documentation.
For a frontier lab, that is documentation work that displaces under a day of research.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `The original 2024 law imposed a duty of care with impact assessments and risk programs. If other states adopt that version, and courts read general-purpose models as covered, the burden grows.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Colorado SB26-189", url: "https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189" },
    { title: "Davis Wright Tremaine on Colorado's repeal and replacement (May 2026)", url: "https://www.dwt.com/blogs/privacy--security-law-blog/2026/05/colorado-ai-act-repeal-new-transparency-law" },
    { title: "Norton Rose Fulbright on xAI's lawsuit (2026)", url: "https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en/knowledge/publications/de3ad9de/xai-sues-doj-intervenes-enforcement-of-colorado-ai-act-suspended" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Mostly a burden on companies using AI, not building it." },

{ id: "liability-insurance", section: "mains", name: "Mandatory liability insurance for AI developers",
  practice: "Frontier AI developers must carry insurance against harms their systems could cause, and the insurer, not the developer, chooses who audits their safety practices.",
  why: "Premiums are money, not time. Underwriting and insurer-chosen audits take some senior time, under a day of progress. The bigger risk is that insurers won't cover AI at all.",
  how: "Our estimate on the model's logic, like product liability: underwriting and insurer audits take senior time but don't stop training. Under a day.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Gabriel Weil, law professor","what":"proposed mandatory insurance with insurer-chosen auditors, 2026.","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/dont-let-ai-developers-hire-their-own-referees"},{"who":"Cristian Trout","what":"argued insurance can overcome developers' judgment-proofness, 2024.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.06673"}],"against":[]},
  short: "Frontier developers must insure against the harms their systems could cause.",
  trigger: "Frontier AI developers.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "cond" },
  d_us: { low: 0.00329, central: 0.01314, high: 0.0657 }, d_access: { low: 0.01971, central: 0.03942, high: 0.06307 },
  recurrence: "Per year",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "Whether insurers would offer coverage at all. Many are currently excluding AI risks from their policies.",
  requires: ["Carry liability insurance covering the insurable layer of harms from frontier systems.", "Insurers, rather than developers, select third-party auditors."],
  baseline: "No U.S. bill requires it. Specialist insurers such as AIUC certify and insure AI agents voluntarily. Many general insurers are adding AI exclusions to their policies.",
  reasoning: `Gabriel Weil proposes mandatory insurance paired with liability, with insurers choosing auditors so developers cannot pick friendly ones. Premiums are dollars, which the rubric does not convert into time. The time cost is the underwriting process and insurer audits. Like product liability in the simulation model, they take senior time but don't stop training: under a day.
CSIS reported in September 2026 that insurers are adding AI exclusions, including an industry-standard exclusion effective January 2026. If no insurer will write the policy, a mandate could block releases entirely, which is the high end of the range.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Catastrophic AI losses could exceed what private insurance can cover. A mandate without available coverage becomes a de facto ban on deploying the most capable models.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Gabriel Weil, Don't let AI developers hire their own referees (AI Frontiers, July 2026)", url: "https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/dont-let-ai-developers-hire-their-own-referees" },
    { title: "CSIS, The insurance industry's retreat from AI (Sept 2026)", url: "https://www.csis.org/analysis/insurance-industrys-retreat-ai-threatens-slow-innovation-and-adoption" },
    { title: "Trout, insurance for AI risk (arXiv)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.06673" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "Premiums are money, not time. Audits and underwriting take a little senior time." },

{ id: "nuclear-control", section: "starters", name: "Human control of nuclear launch decisions",
  practice: "Only people, not AI systems, can decide to use nuclear weapons. The U.S. and China affirmed this in 2024, and U.S. law states it as policy.",
  why: "It governs the military, not AI labs, so it costs America's AI lead nothing.",
  how: "No cost to AI developers.",
  basis: "estimate",
  advocates: {"for":[{"who":"Sen. Ed Markey and Rep. Ted Lieu","what":"said humans must be in the loop of any nuclear launch, 2024.","url":"https://markey.senate.gov/news/press-releases/markey-lieu-applaud-inclusion-of-human-in-the-loop-nuclear-launch-safeguard"},{"who":"Friends Committee on National Legislation","what":"urged Congress to prohibit AI in nuclear launch decisions, 2026.","url":"https://www.fcnl.org/resources/recommendations-fy2027-ndaa"}],"against":[]},
  short: "Only humans may decide to launch nuclear weapons.",
  trigger: "U.S. nuclear command and control.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 }, d_access: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "High", evidence: "high",
  uncertainty: "None for the lead.",
  requires: ["Require positive human action in executing presidential decisions on nuclear weapons (FY2025 NDAA, section 1638, as a statement of policy).", "Affirm human control over nuclear use decisions internationally (2024 Biden–Xi statement)."],
  baseline: "U.S. policy since the FY2025 defense bill. A stricter funding ban has not been enacted.",
  reasoning: `This is one of the most widely supported AI safety measures and one of the few the U.S. and China have jointly affirmed. It applies to the Defense Department and the President, not to AI developers.
It costs America's AI lead nothing.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `There is no plausible case that it slows frontier AI. The debate is whether a statement of policy is strong enough.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "White House readout of the Biden–Xi meeting (Nov 16, 2024)", url: "https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2024/11/16/readout-of-president-joe-bidens-meeting-with-president-xi-jinping-of-the-peoples-republic-of-china-3" },
    { title: "FY2025 NDAA, section 1638", url: "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-118publ159/html/PLAW-118publ159.htm" }
  ],
  quiz_hint: "It governs the military, not AI labs." },

{ id: "weight-security", section: "sides", name: "Model-weight security standard",
  practice: "Labs must protect unreleased models from theft by skilled attackers, including foreign spies, with strict access controls and monitoring.",
  why: "Stronger protection costs under a day of U.S. progress. It makes a stolen U.S. model less likely, which on balance slightly widens the lead.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "RAND", "what": "defined five security levels for protecting model weights, up to nation-state attackers, 2024.", "url": "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html"}, {"who": "Anthropic", "what": "activated ASL-3 protections focused on securing model weights, 2025.", "url": "https://www.anthropic.com/news/activating-asl3-protections"}, {"who": "Institute for Progress", "what": "proposed a national sprint to reach the highest security level, 2025.", "url": "https://ifp.org/a-sprint-toward-security-level-5/"}, {"who": "California SB 53", "what": "requires frameworks to cover security of unreleased model weights, 2025.", "url": "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China falls under a day directly; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/weight-security.html",
  short: "Protect unreleased frontier weights against theft by well-resourced attackers.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers holding unreleased model weights.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0061, high: 0.01016 }, d_access: { low: 0.00077, central: 0.0023, high: 0.00525 },
  d_china: { low: 0.00656, central: 0.01961, high: 0.04483 }, theft_cut: 0.201,
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "The probability that frontier weights get stolen in a given year, which nobody outside the labs and intelligence agencies can estimate.",
  requires: ["Implement security controls for unreleased model weights at a defined level (RAND's SL3, roughly: resistant to well-resourced non-state and moderately resourced state actors).", "Describe the controls in the published framework (SB 53 requires this)."],
  baseline: "Anthropic's ASL-3 standard and OpenAI's security commitments already target this level. SB 53 requires large developers to describe their weight-security practices. The mandate sets a floor under current practice.",
  reasoning: `An SL3-like floor is incremental to existing voluntary hardening. Hazard reduction, current asset value, assimilation and decay determine the benefit. Research effort and ongoing friction determine the U.S. cost. The compromise rate and effectiveness are explicit priors.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is under a day behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, under a day, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `A higher price would argue that security standards, once regulated, ratchet toward the paranoid: access controls that slow every engineer, air-gapped clusters and clearance requirements. That is the next item on the menu. At the SL3 level, the honest steelman is that the China-side benefit is speculative; it may be zero if theft was never the channel.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "RAND, Securing AI Model Weights", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html" },
    { title: "Anthropic Responsible Scaling Policy", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy" },
    { title: "California Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.12 (SB 53): cybersecurity for unreleased weights in the framework", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=22757.12" }
  ] },

{ id: "secure-dev-env", section: "sides", name: "Isolated secure development environment",
  practice: "Frontier research moves onto isolated computer networks with tight physical security, much like classified government work.",
  why: "Isolation slows U.S. researchers, about 6 days of progress a year in. It also blocks theft and spying, slowing China by about 3 days. Net: about 3 days, and highly uncertain.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Leopold Aschenbrenner", "what": "called for fully air-gapped datacenters and research staff working in secure facilities, 2024.", "url": "https://situational-awareness.ai/lock-down-the-labs/"}, {"who": "Sella Nevo, Institute for Progress", "what": "proposed a government-led push to top-level security including air-gapped access, 2025.", "url": "https://ifp.org/a-sprint-toward-security-level-5/"}, {"who": "SL5 Standard authors", "what": "defined security able to thwart the world's most capable cyber institutions, 2026.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08449"}], "against": []},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up about 6 days behind where it would be; China falls about 3 days directly; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/secure-dev-env.html",
  short: "Frontier research on air-gapped or tightly isolated networks with physical security controls.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers, for models above a capability threshold.",
  clocks: { training: "cond", internal: "cond", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.09204, central: 0.20107, high: 0.33926 }, d_access: { low: 0.01696, central: 0.05422, high: 0.08363 },
  d_china: { low: 0.03051, central: 0.09757, high: 0.15048 }, theft_cut: 1.000,
  recurrence: "Per year of operation",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "The productivity penalty on researchers. Anthropic is measuring it now and has not published a number.",
  requires: ["Conduct frontier training and research inside isolated networks (RAND SL4 to SL5) with limited, controlled external connections and commensurate physical security.", "Restrict researcher access and remote work accordingly."],
  baseline: "No lab operates this way. Anthropic's Frontier Safety Roadmap says its initial discussions leave it skeptical that isolated networks are feasible within one to two years, and it is running a small-scale mock secure research environment to measure the productivity impact.",
  reasoning: `The model allows internal AI tools inside isolated environments. Research friction applies only to newly affected work; migration is additional. A policy that prohibited such tools or required immediate construction of an entirely new facility would have a different scope.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is about 6 days behind its baseline path and China's is about 3 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 3 days, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `Every researcher who has worked in a SCIF says the same thing: it is slower. If the penalty is 30% and it compounds across a field where the frontier doubles every 105 days, the U.S. hands away a third of its own progress to protect against a threat it cannot quantify. Anthropic's own roadmap says the isolated-network version may not be feasible at all on the relevant timescale.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Anthropic Frontier Safety Roadmap updates (May 5, 2026)", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy/updates", note: "skeptical isolated networks are feasible in 1–2 years; mock secure research environment to measure productivity impact" },
    { title: "Anthropic Frontier Safety Roadmap", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy/roadmap" },
    { title: "RAND, Securing AI Model Weights", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html", note: "security levels SL1–SL5" }
  ] },

{ id: "kyc-compute", section: "sides", name: "Know-your-customer rules for compute providers",
  practice: "Cloud companies must check the identity of any customer renting enough computing power to train a frontier model.",
  why: "It costs U.S. labs almost nothing and closes a route for Chinese labs to rent Western chips, worth about 2 days of lead.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Commerce Department", "what": "proposed know-your-customer rules for U.S. cloud providers, 2024.", "url": "https://www.bis.gov/press-release/commerce-proposes-rule-advance-u.s.-national-security-interests-implement-biden-harris-administrations-ai"}, {"who": "Janet Egan and Lennart Heim, GovAI", "what": "argued compute providers should run KYC schemes, 2023.", "url": "https://www.governance.ai/research-paper/oversight-for-frontier-ai-through-kyc-scheme-for-compute-providers"}, {"who": "CSET, Georgetown", "what": "called customer identification an important step for national security, 2024.", "url": "https://cset.georgetown.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024.04.29-CSET-IaaS-KYC-RFI-Response.pdf"}], "against": [{"who": "U.S. Chamber of Commerce", "what": "called cloud KYC costly, burdensome and ineffective, 2024.", "url": "https://www.uschamber.com/security/cybersecurity/u-s-chamber-of-commerce-eo-13984-nprm-comments-to-commerce-department"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China falls about 2 days directly; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/kyc-compute.html",
  short: "Cloud providers verify who is renting enough compute to train a frontier model.",
  trigger: "Compute providers serving customers at frontier scale.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00434, high: 0.00778 }, d_access: { low: 0.00018, central: 0.00087, high: 0.00226 },
  d_china: { low: 0.01164, central: 0.05618, high: 0.14582 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "low",
  uncertainty: "How much Chinese frontier training currently runs on rented Western compute.",
  requires: ["Collect identity, payment and contact information from any customer using enough compute to train a covered model; assess intent; retain records (SB 1047 § 22604).", "Implement the ability to shut down a customer's resources."],
  baseline: "The 2024 Commerce Department proposed KYC rules for infrastructure-as-a-service providers. Major clouds already do sanctions screening. SB 1047 would have required it in California.",
  reasoning: `The base case allows new identity checks to uncover access prohibited by existing rules. Identity verification alone does not deny compute: the zero-denial boundary has zero China hardware benefit. This scope is narrower than a new blanket compute embargo.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is about 2 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 2 days, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `KYC on compute is an export control in disguise and shares its weakness: it only works if the covered providers are the only providers. A higher U.S. price would come from pushing customers to non-U.S. clouds, which is a commercial cost and not a lead cost.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "SB 1047 enrolled text, § 22604: compute cluster operator duties", url: "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB1047" },
    { title: "Commerce/BIS proposed rule: KYC for IaaS providers (Jan 2024)", url: "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/01/29/2024-01580/taking-additional-steps-to-address-the-national-emergency-with-respect-to-significant-malicious-cyber-enabled" }
  ] },

{ id: "anti-distillation", section: "offmenu", name: "Anti-distillation controls",
  practice: "Labs must verify large API customers, hide models' full reasoning, and watch for accounts that copy model outputs to train competing models.",
  why: "It costs U.S. labs a little product polish. The benefit depends entirely on how much China depends on U.S. models: none if it doesn't, about 4 days at \"a little.\"",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "OpenAI", "what": "told Congress DeepSeek keeps trying to distill U.S. models and asked for government help, 2026.", "url": "https://assets.bwbx.io/documents/users/iqjWHBFdfxIU/rRmql_jJcxb4/v0"}, {"who": "Anthropic", "what": "named DeepSeek, Moonshot and MiniMax and said no company can solve this alone, 2026.", "url": "https://www.anthropic.com/news/detecting-and-preventing-distillation-attacks"}, {"who": "House Select Committee on the CCP", "what": "found it highly likely DeepSeek used unlawful distillation, 2025.", "url": "https://chinaselectcommittee.house.gov/media/reports/deepseek-unmasked-exposing-the-ccp-s-latest-tool-for-spying-stealing-and-subverting-us-export-control-restrictions"}, {"who": "NSA, CISA and FBI", "what": "named six Chinese labs and recommended detection and information sharing, 2026.", "url": "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa26-251a"}], "against": [{"who": "Tory Green, IO.Net", "what": "mocked labs that train on the open internet and then complain about distillation, 2026.", "url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/critics-mock-anthropics-claims-chinese-224040472.html"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China loses about 4 days more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/anti-distillation.html",
  short: "KYC for high-volume API access, hidden reasoning traces, output fingerprinting and rate limits.",
  trigger: "Frontier developers offering API access.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: 0.0, central: 0.00777, high: 0.01368 }, d_access: { low: 0.12537, central: 0.52116, high: 1.27062 },
  d_china: { low: 0.0, central: 0.0, high: 0.0 },
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "How much of Chinese progress actually came from distilling U.S. outputs, which is the same thing your worldview is about.",
  requires: ["Verify identity for high-volume API customers and enforce terms against training on outputs.", "Do not expose full reasoning traces by default.", "Fingerprint outputs and share attack telemetry across labs and with government."],
  baseline: "Anthropic reported over 16 million exchanges through about 24,000 fraudulent accounts in February 2026 and, in September, the largest distillation campaign it had measured, attributed to Alibaba, with over 151 million exchanges. OpenAI told a House committee it observed DeepSeek obtaining outputs through obfuscated third-party routers. A joint NSA, CISA and FBI advisory in September 2026 named six Chinese labs. Labs are already tightening; a mandate standardizes it.",
  reasoning: `The same marginal teacher-reliance parameter is used here and for public-release delays. Measured benefits from distillation establish that teachers can help; they do not establish how much China needs U.S. teachers after domestic alternatives and self-training.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is about 4 days behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 4 days, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `A higher U.S. price: hidden reasoning traces and heavy KYC degrade the product for legitimate developers, and U.S. open-weight releases would need the same treatment or the controls are pointless. A lower China price: DeepSeek V4's model card describes distilling from its own specialist models, and the behavioral evidence of U.S. lineage is suggestive rather than quantified. Nobody has shown how many months distillation bought.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Anthropic, Detecting and preventing distillation attacks (Feb 23, 2026)", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/news/detecting-and-preventing-distillation-attacks" },
    { title: "Anthropic Threat Intelligence Report, September 2026", url: "https://www.anthropic.com/threat-intelligence-report-september-2026", note: "Alibaba campaign: over 151 million exchanges" },
    { title: "NSA/CISA/FBI advisory AA26-251A (Sept 8, 2026)", url: "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa26-251a" },
    { title: "OpenAI memo to the House Select Committee on the CCP (Feb 2026)", url: "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/openai-accuses-deepseek-distilling-us-221629899.html", secondary: "Reuters" },
    { title: "Busbridge et al., Distillation Scaling Laws (ICML 2025)", url: "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v267/busbridge25a.html" }
  ] },

{ id: "export-controls", section: "offmenu", name: "Advanced chip export controls",
  practice: "The U.S. bans sales of advanced AI chips and chipmaking tools to China.",
  why: "Keeping the ban, rather than relaxing it now, keeps China short of chips it would otherwise buy over the year. That is worth about four weeks of lead.",
  advocates: {"for": [{"who": "Commerce Department", "what": "restricted China's access to advanced chips and chipmaking tools, 2022.", "url": "https://www.bis.gov/press-release/commerce-implements-new-export-controls-advanced-computing-semiconductor-manufacturing-items-peoples"}, {"who": "Anthropic", "what": "said keeping America's compute advantage through export controls is essential, 2025.", "url": "https://www.anthropic.com/news/securing-america-s-compute-advantage-anthropic-s-position-on-the-diffusion-rule"}, {"who": "Institute for Progress", "what": "argued against selling Nvidia's Hopper chips to China, 2025.", "url": "https://ifp.org/should-the-us-sell-hopper-chips-to-china/"}], "against": [{"who": "Jensen Huang, Nvidia", "what": "called the export controls a failure and precisely the wrong policy, 2025.", "url": "https://www.theregister.com/2025/05/21/jensen_huang_h20_ban_criticism/"}]},
  basis: "model",
  how: "Simulation model, 12 months after the rule takes effect: U.S. progress ends up under a day behind where it would be; China falls about 4 weeks directly; China loses under a day more through its dependence on U.S. models, at the \"a little\" setting. Most of the model's inputs are stated assumptions.",
  model_page: "appendix/info/export-controls.html",
  short: "Not a safety rule. On the menu so you can see what buying lead looks like.",
  trigger: "Exports of advanced AI chips and manufacturing equipment to China.",
  clocks: { training: "no", internal: "no", release: "no" },
  d_us: { low: -0.05167, central: -0.02547, high: -0.00855 }, d_access: { low: -0.06545, central: -0.03969, high: 0.00022 },
  d_china: { low: 0.30309, central: 0.86399, high: 1.7328 },
  recurrence: "Per year the controls hold",
  confidence: "Low", evidence: "medium",
  uncertainty: "Whether compute is the binding constraint on Chinese frontier progress. The gap is measured in months while the hardware gap is measured in years, which suggests it is not the only constraint.",
  requires: ["Prohibit export of advanced AI accelerators and the tools to make them to China.", "Enforce against transshipment."],
  baseline: "In force since October 2022, tightened repeatedly. Estimates: a U.S. hardware lead of about four years (Epoch), Chinese domestic chips trailing by three to four years through 2030 (Epoch on Huawei), U.S. compute capacity roughly ten times China's (RAND).",
  reasoning: `This maintains the specified controls versus prospective relaxation today. It preserves existing stocks, allows deployment lags, and models the extra hardware China would actually buy and use. It does not convert a four-year chip-generation lag directly into a national capability lag.
In the model, a year after the rule takes effect, U.S. capability is under a day behind its baseline path and China's is about 3.5 weeks behind at the model's central assumption about dependence. The difference, about 4 weeks, is the model's central estimate. The full calculation, every input and its evidence status are on this rule's page in the technical appendix.`,
  rubric: [],
  steelman: `For a lower China price: DeepSeek trained V3 on a fraction of the compute U.S. labs used and the gap did not widen. For a higher price: Epoch projects Huawei's output at under 4% of Nvidia's in 2026 and a 73-times gap in usable high-bandwidth memory, and inference-time compute is now a large part of capability. Both cases are sourced. Neither is settled.`,
  sources: [
    { title: "Epoch AI, U.S. export controls and China's AI progress", url: "https://epoch.ai/gradient-updates/us-export-controls-china-ai" },
    { title: "Epoch AI, Huawei's roadmap to 2031", url: "https://epoch.ai/publications/huaweis-roadmap-to-2031" },
    { title: "RAND (Heim), China's AI models are closing the gap", url: "https://www.rand.org/pubs/commentary/2025/05/chinas-ai-models-are-closing-the-gap-but-americas-real.html" },
    { title: "CSIS, China's open-weight challenge to U.S. AI leadership", url: "https://www.csis.org/analysis/chinas-open-weight-challenge-us-ai-leadership" },
    { title: "AI Frontiers (Chris Miller), U.S. chip export controls and China", url: "https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/us-chip-export-controls-china-ai" }
  ] }

